1795115974472072	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1uq	{"description":"A destination may list names; permits then needs --name with one of them. For addresses shared with other parties (CDN, shared hosting).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Optional per-destination names restriction (narrows, never grants)"}	blake3:JiEhHzhEGB2L-d0QZp9G9W_olJulLqQdfiQa-X9TqyY
1795115974497553	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-1uq	{"state":"done"}	blake3:xPHg_mi80MZl0GinApu0n5MtwbRPXB4PfrL15PNWDiQ
1795186102273896	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-34a	{"description":"sippycup's call ledger reserves every call of a command before the first packet and releases, with a reason, each call for which no INVITE can have left (preflight failed, registration failed, step never started). hallpass has spends only: a count never goes down. Today a caller must spend at the moment of first send (hallpass spend --to, one call at a time), which gives 'a count from first send' but no up-front refusal of a whole matrix. Decide: (a) stay spend-only and document it (current state, docs/INTEGRATING.md), or (b) add a release line to the ledger chain (seq of the spend, amount given back, reason; never below zero; shown by hallpass ledger; carried through adoption). (b) is the first thing in hallpass that makes a count smaller, so it is the owner's decision, not a papercut.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Release a spend whose traffic never left (a caller that reserves before its first packet)"}	blake3:CAbbwi2sAi4dHoX0ta-3UyEEWwdknNwcjtXBMhlVlKk
1795186102369079	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1na	{"description":"sippycup's ready, rehearse, dry runs and plans read the same rule as the real reservation and print the same text, without counting. hallpass has no such call: status --format json gives counters[].remaining and permits gives the destination and window, but a caller has to combine them itself and the refusal texts differ from spend's. Add spend --dry-run (library: a flag on SpendRequest or a would_spend function) that takes the same locks for reading, decides exactly as spend does (digest, window, destination, counter, ceiling, ledger and witness state) and records nothing; JSON result would-allow/refused. Must fail closed on everything spend fails closed on, including an unusable witness.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Ask whether a spend would fit without recording it (dry run with the same decision path)"}	blake3:4RyHPH7qu8rPn0VtNzmEkz_qzPgk0QTxhenE9ufBJNo
1795186102460703	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2qt	{"description":"sippycup's practice benches reuse one approval reference in many directories by design (BENCH_APPROVAL_IDS: LOCAL-PRACTICE-ONLY, LOCAL-PRACTICE-REHEARSAL, ROUTED-REHEARSAL), and practice-target up records a new approval window with a fresh budget on every start. hallpass refuses a second pass file with a known id (ledger-missing, ledger-changed) and never resets a count. No hallpass change is needed if each bench start writes a new pass with its own id (for example practice-20261120T090000Z) for loopback destinations only; then the witness directory grows by one small file per start. Decide whether that is the answer, or whether hallpass needs a marked bench pass (and if so, how it is kept from ever naming a non-loopback destination).","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Bench passes: one id used in many directories, and a new window and budget on each start"}	blake3:F97bS3qgL2TfSDab6TQz1X75coV69RQ_xGyZ1p96oWM
1795186102551410	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ql	{"description":"sippycup approval ids are 1-128 printable characters (CHG-1234, but also text with spaces); a hallpass id is 1-64 of letters, digits, . _ -, and is also a file name in the witness directory. sippycup also accepts an approval with validUntil only; hallpass requires valid_from. A caller that writes passes from sippycup scope files needs one documented rule: where the owner's reference goes (approval.reference keeps it verbatim, up to 200 characters), how the id is derived so that the same approval always gets the same id (per-approval counts depend on it), and what valid_from is when the owner gave none (hallpass should keep requiring it: the operator writes the time the approval was given).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Map an owner's approval reference to a pass id, and approvals that state no start"}	blake3:aQotOtCbez2DAKAueu6VFHMbEwJsTPlPNELFlWVhvTw
1795186102645925	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1du	{"description":"A hallpass ledger entry holds counter, amount, time, pass digest and a free note of 500 characters. sippycup's reserve entries hold the command, the source file and a reservation id that each run directory points back to, and its cross-check accounts for every run directory. With spend --to the decision now includes the destination, but the entry does not say which. Adding fields is a ledger format change: older hallpass versions fail closed on unknown fields (as they do on adoption lines). Until then a caller can put its own run id in --note and store the returned seq in its run directory. Decide the fields (to, name, label, caller, ref) and the version rule.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Record what a spend paid for in its ledger entry (target, caller, run reference)"}	blake3:nz-1DJpWP1o-rOpxMPUEW9YMIBCz7lqbHsgndAUTNQY
1795186102739742	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3z6	{"description":"sippycup's media guard checks every signalling and media address of a call (findings 3 and 17: no RTP or RTCP leaves for an unlisted media address). With the CLI that is one process start per address: permits for each media address, then spend --to for the signalling address. clap refuses a repeated --to today (exit 1), which is the safe behaviour. Add repeated --to on permits and spend (all must be allowed; the refusal names the first one that is not; JSON targets as a list) so one call covers one unit of work. Library callers can already loop over Pass::permits under one load.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Several targets in one question (signalling, RTP and RTCP addresses of one call)"}	blake3:Ih1N87zeORfaRQDqn5ysbBQhctOO746wJzs_3eklvlI
1795186102830112	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-12w	{"description":"sippycup limits authorization.networks in total: at most one IPv4 /8 and one IPv6 /64 across all networks. hallpass caps each destination (/24 and /64, or /8 and /32 with whole_network_approved) but not their sum: 1024 destinations of /8 each validate. Decide a total (addresses per family across all destinations, with whole_network_approved counted) and report it in check and status.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"A total breadth rule across destinations"}	blake3:0YUvXpRj4YcyR9mkETJ2klJ8Y4ChfYoaeg8M3Ouf9WE
1795186102923580	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13z	{"description":"The window uses the caller's clock. A clock set back reopens an expired window, and nothing notices: spend does not compare now with the time of the last ledger entry (tests/cli.rs even spends at 10:00 and then at 09:00). A plain refusal is not certain: two processes take their clocks before waiting for the lock, so entries are legitimately out of order by the lock wait, and one entry written with a clock far in the future would lock the pass until then. Decide a tolerance and a recovery (for example: refuse when now is more than N seconds before the last entry, with hallpass adopt as the recorded way past it).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Notice a clock that went backwards (now is earlier than the last ledger entry)"}	blake3:ortGkHWfPsjiTEj4jn4BeG8i4jUCHze2oI87S9eioAM
1795186103015134	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-649	{"description":"spend takes now from the caller (the CLI reads the clock at start) and then waits for the witness and ledger locks without limit. If another process holds the lock for long (stopped, or a slow file system), the spend is decided for the instant before the wait and can be recorded after valid_until. --duration covers this when the caller's duration includes the wait. Options: try_lock with a bounded wait and a clear error; or let the CLI re-read the clock after the lock (needs a clock callback in the library, which today takes now as a value on purpose).","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Bound the wait for the ledger lock, or take the clock again after it"}	blake3:Q_YKn0N2jGlsLOnNWorb8XYqv8HeHeqizCsRO5eO1Bc
1795186103105841	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2rf	{"description":"By design (DESIGN.md, 'State directory failures') the read-only commands answer with a warning and witness: not-checked when the state directory or witness is unusable: permits then says allowed, exit 0, with the reason in warnings. spend fails closed in the same situation, and a caller that follows docs/INTEGRATING.md sends only after spend. A caller that uses permits alone (no ceilings) gets an allowed while a state file is unreadable. Either keep it (the destination and window answer does not depend on the count) or make permits exit 1 like spend; tests/cli.rs an_unusable_state_directory_fails_closed_for_spends pins today's behaviour.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Decide whether permits should refuse when the witness cannot be checked"}	blake3:fFI-pLsomXmf_mflvnmn-ag9B9m0bvh7JbQDCYAmaTw
1795186103195199	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1my	{"description":"DESIGN.md and PASS-FORMAT.md speak of what 'hallpass 0.1' did and what holds 'since 0.2' (notes, adoption lines, the witness), but Cargo.toml is still 0.1.0, and hallpass --version prints that. An integrating tool cannot tell which behaviour it is calling. Bump the version, and consider a hallpass field in every JSON object (or document that fields are only ever added).","kind":"task","size":"xs","title":"Version drift: the crate says 0.1.0, the docs describe 0.2 behaviour, and JSON output carries no version"}	blake3:0qaas3qp2U3CL7DzLwRxaz1uCFcl2e1r3ScjvMVaPkM
1795186103290317	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-210	{"description":"load refuses a pass that group or others may write, but does not look at who owns it: a pass in a shared directory that belongs to another user and has mode 0644 is read as the operator's own approval, and that user can change it. Comparing the file's uid with the uid of the process needs geteuid (libc, or the owner of /proc/self), which the crate has avoided so far. Decide whether to add the check (and the dependency).","kind":"task","size":"xs","title":"A pass owned by another user is accepted when its mode is 0644"}	blake3:0gCLVXfL50vwrBLZcX2MXCpODJ5_iEBIpxwZHDU0XEk
1795198143746272	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1cu	{"description":"An operator who pinned a pass can tell that the bytes changed, but has to read a TOML diff to decide what to take back to the owner. Give the reviewer a field-level account of the change before they accept a new digest.\n\nDelivery\nAdd a read-only diff OLD NEW command backed by a library comparison of two LoadedPass values. Print both byte digests. Compare approval identity, UTC window, revocation, ceilings and destinations keyed by label. Normalize port ranges and name sets for comparison. List introduced/removed labels and changes of match order explicitly. Treat an ID change as a different approval and ledger identity.\n\nAcceptance\n- A comment edit changes the digest and reports equivalent parsed fields. An equivalent port range and offset spelling produce no scope change.\n- Removing a name restriction, extending the window, restoring a revoked pass or raising a ceiling each gets an explicit before/after row. Mixed and overlapping edits get a review-required classification whenever whole-pass containment is uncertain.\n- An ID change is prominent even when all rules match. Reordering overlapping labels reports its possible effect on the returned label. An invalid input identifies its side and produces no comparison result.\n- Cover text and JSON in tests/cli.rs and document that diff never authorizes a spend. Use src/pass.rs and src/ports.rs. V1 compares declared rules and makes no claim to solve arbitrary policy equivalence.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Compare approval revisions and flag edits that need owner review"}	blake3:7FHfXiE9BJw39KmOCtIqZW6_6OnhzrlcH6F2Z-reWLY
1795198144674292	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2vs	{"description":"A reviewer can read a destination's names list and reasonably expect it to constrain the service. Pass::permits_named accepts a query when any matching destination allows it. Another entry can therefore make the apparent restriction ineffective on part of the address and port range.\n\nDelivery\nAdd an advisory lint with a stable finding code, both labels and the overlapping address, protocol and port range. Start with a named rule overlapping a nameless rule. Use Network::overlaps, inclusive port intersection and the existing decision path. Bound or group output on large passes and signal truncation. Expose findings through a read-only CLI operation and JSON.\n\nAcceptance\n- A fixture with two overlapping rules shows exactly which service portion also accepts an unnamed query. Disjoint address families, transports and ports produce no finding.\n- A finding described as an effective permission includes one literal example validated through permits_named at an in-window instant on a non-revoked fixture. Distinguish a structural overlap on a revoked pass from a live permission. Account for IPv4 network edges and explicit host entries.\n- Reordering the rules preserves the finding. Existing check, permits and spend outcomes stay compatible. Add fixture-based tests and a reviewer example in docs/PASS-FORMAT.md.\n- Scope is the interaction between name restrictions and overlapping rules. Aggregate network breadth is already tracked in bn-12w.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Flag overlapping destinations that weaken an approved name restriction"}	blake3:tRhaloqZ6h4BQuj79P57l2CCzsTO2kpJhja0lIN_y5w
1795198145569180	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2uf	{"description":"A counted unit can require further read-only target checks. spend accepts the digest confirmed by the operator, while the documented follow-up permits invocation reloads the current pass without a digest precondition. Give callers a single way to keep a whole unit on the approved revision.\n\nDelivery\nAdd permits --digest and a path-based Rust operation accepting an optional expected Digest, explicit StateDir, target, name, instant and duration. Route the CLI through it. Load once per call, apply the existing ledger/witness checks, compare the expected digest with that LoadedPass, then decide against the very same Pass. Preserve pure in-memory methods.\n\nAcceptance\n- With a matching pin, existing named/unnamed decisions, warnings and exit codes are preserved. A stale valid pin gives pass-changed and exit 2 on an otherwise valid state, even for a comment edit. An invalid digest is a usage error.\n- A corrupt ledger remains an error. Preserve the existing read-only witness policy, including unavailable-state warnings (bn-2rf owns changes to that policy).\n- A CLI integration example spends against revision A, edits the pass while retaining its ID, and refuses the secondary check pinned to A. A call without a pin follows current behavior. Neither check appends a ledger entry.\n- Cover the same cases in the Rust path and update docs/INTEGRATING.md. No second spend is needed for a follow-up address.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Pin follow-up target checks to the pass revision confirmed at run start"}	blake3:IbV5iSYEakGsYHgqDO-EgM-ASmLBs0VAlAgdqN8qZTo
1795198146339522	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-uwj	{"description":"An owner can describe the boundaries of an approval in examples that should survive subsequent edits. A mismatched expectation should be visible in review, including when the surprising result is an allowance.\n\nDelivery\nAdd an offline test-pass PASS CASES command and a small versioned case format. Each named case supplies its own offset timestamp, target, optional name and duration, and expected allowance or stable refusal code. Parse through the shipping target/name parsers and evaluate with Pass::permits_named. Use one loaded pass and include its digest in the report.\n\nAcceptance\n- Demonstrate a permitted named service, an unapproved name, and an exclusive window-end case. Editing the pass so the negative example succeeds flags just that case as a mismatch.\n- Report expected and actual outcomes for every valid case, in input order. Define JSON and exits explicitly: 0 all expectations match, 2 mismatches, 1 malformed input or invalid pass. Reject empty suites, duplicate case IDs and unknown fields.\n- Run fixtures with the release binary, no test clock and an empty temporary home. Verify the state directory and ledger were never created.\n- Document a bounded input size/case count and a runnable suite in docs/INTEGRATING.md. This exercises declared policy at supplied instants. Spend previews against live totals remain bn-1na.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Let owners regression-test a pass with written allow and refuse examples"}	blake3:ln0HUkSw0UdixBg2-G7PDjdGRRjMTMkgPOPIj9XiawM
1795198146676385	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bz	{"description":"One operation may cost one request and a known byte allowance. Two spend calls can consume the first budget and then fail on the second. A single accounting decision should either record the whole cost or leave every total alone.\n\nDelivery\nAdd a Rust multi-charge request and an unambiguous CLI form with one target and one set of shared preconditions. Require distinct counters, positive bounded amounts and at most MAX_COUNTERS charges. Under the existing lock order validate the digest, window, destination and every ceiling, then append and sync one chain record with one sequence. Keep the single-counter API and its on-disk representation for ordinary spends.\n\nAcceptance\n- With requests=1 and bytes=100, a request for (1,101) records no debit. A subsequent (1,100) succeeds once. Concurrent workers racing for either last unit cannot split or exceed totals.\n- Duplicate/unknown counters, overflow, and empty lists produce documented outcomes. Reopen the ledger and confirm status, ledger filtering and adoption carry-forward account for each charge exactly once.\n- Test old spend and adoption fixtures, mixed old/new records and a cut final record. Older readers must fail closed on the new record. Specify the wire format, version rule, JSON breakdown and the existing committed-but-witness-update-failed behavior before exposing the CLI.\n- Coordinate ledger representation with bn-1du if both land. Multiple targets per decision belong to bn-3z6. Keep v1 to known costs with no release/refund lifecycle.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"l","title":"Charge several approved counters in one all-or-none spend"}	blake3:sE5IuXNDlpFLXurclmMcaVkZO5IE1ujZfD5wY2AkD8U
1795198147236234	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-11g	{"description":"An owner who keeps a closeout or handoff report needs to check later whether the ledger still contains the exact history they reviewed, even in a separate account with no local witness. The chain already has a compact commitment to that history.\n\nDelivery\nAdd offline checkpoint export and verification for an existing ledger. Export a versioned object with pass ID, sequence and hash of the exact line at that sequence (the header for seq 0). Verify the whole supplied ledger, including later lines, then classify an exact match, a valid extension, a shorter ledger or a different history. Reuse src/ledger.rs parsing and byte-hashing conventions.\n\nAcceptance\n- Checkpoint after two spends, copy it to a separate temporary directory, append another spend and verify the copy as a valid extension with one later line. The original two-entry file verifies as exact.\n- Edited or missing checkpointed history, a malformed suffix, a different pass ID and a missing ledger cannot verify. A header-only ledger has a defined checkpoint. A pass edit keeping its ID does not by itself change recorded history.\n- Export fails on unusable or conflicting local evidence and states when local corroboration is absent. Standalone verification reports its supplied checkpoint as the trust input and never creates or adopts a witness.\n- Document text/JSON outcomes and external retention. A checkpoint copied from the same rewritten files cannot establish independent provenance. Omit notes, paths and owner contact details from the object.","kind":"task","labels":["audit"],"size":"m","title":"Verify a ledger against a checkpoint kept with an engagement report"}	blake3:dvoXxrHgIX7QDn86-QxgSfy4pvNIfQBUm8hvUY0lVIM
1795198147482867	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-k5s	{"description":"Before adopting a lost or replaced ledger, an operator should see exactly what counts will be carried forward. Today adopt computes that information on the write path and prints it after appending.\n\nDelivery\nAdd adopt --preview with a side-effect-free inspection path. Extract the per-counter carry calculation from adopt so preview and apply use it. Show adoption case, pass digest, witness and ledger sequences, existing totals, carried delta and resulting totals. Return a token identifying the pass, ledger presence/tail and witness snapshot reviewed. Accept the token as an optional guard on the real adoption.\n\nAcceptance\n- With witnessed totals of six calls and a restored ledger containing four, preview shows four present, two carried and six resulting. A missing ledger previews carrying all six without creating the ledger, state directory or lock files.\n- Cover no witness, fresh state, ledger already matching, corrupt chain and unreadable witness. Only currently adoptable states yield an applicable plan.\n- Recheck the token under the writer locks before creating a replacement ledger or appending. A concurrent spend, changed pass or replaced witness rejects the stale plan without advancing totals or installing a new ledger.\n- Exercise preview then apply through the CLI under temporary state, including two competing applies. The winner appends one adoption. Preserve existing adopt --reason behavior and reason requirement for the write.","kind":"task","labels":["recovery"],"size":"m","title":"Preview adoption and reject recovery if the reviewed state changes"}	blake3:AWmZRIOwkX-FcKrQFGKaxpGMjpH2-DenunCNKvIEeEw
1795198147882830	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3w0	{"description":"A historical spend names a SHA-256 digest. After a pass is edited, the ledger alone cannot show an owner which window, ceilings and names that digest represented. Allow explicit retention of the approved revision at review time.\n\nDelivery\nAdd an opt-in archive command taking a pass and an explicit private directory. Read one bounded byte buffer through src/files.rs, validate and hash that same buffer, then publish it atomically as a private file named from the digest. Add read-only lookup by full digest that verifies the stored bytes before returning the revision. Retrieval is for review and cannot update the live pass or its accounting state.\n\nAcceptance\n- Archive A, record a spend, edit only a comment and archive B. Looking up the spend digest returns A byte for byte including comments. Looking up B returns its exact bytes.\n- Archiving A twice or concurrently is idempotent. A conflicting pre-existing file, symlink, insecure directory, invalid pass, or damaged lookup result gives an error without overwriting anything. Missing digests are explicit.\n- Test a revision change during archive with either one self-consistent snapshot or an error. Ordinary spends never depend on the archive. Output never quietly materializes a recovered pass beside a live ledger.\n- Document local retention of sensitive approval text and show retrieval using a digest from ledger JSON. V1 uses an explicit local directory and needs neither automatic history capture nor a remote store.","kind":"task","labels":["audit"],"size":"m","title":"Archive the exact reviewed pass bytes and retrieve them by ledger digest"}	blake3:IxzUv0CNxW3xoWX04YCYrWLNCj7rOeQsA5H4ZGEfgtU
1795198148662640	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-d6g	{"description":"The worked example in tests/integrating.rs checks the JSON Hallpass produces. It cannot tell an integrating team whether its own subprocess wrapper accidentally treats help output, malformed JSON or a contradictory exit code as permission. Give teams fixtures that exercise their decoder in their own CI.\n\nDelivery\nPublish a small language-neutral, versioned corpus of synthetic process outcomes (stdout, exit code or spawn failure, and expected client action) plus a minimal reference adapter in the integrating docs. Include one successful counted decision and one refusal captured from the real CLI. Use synthetic fixtures for transport or process failures which a healthy binary cannot emit. Drive a callback spy in the example so each case has an observable result without network I/O.\n\nAcceptance\n- Only the valid production-mode spend response advances the example callback. Explicitly cover exit 0 with help, nonzero with allowed JSON, refused/error, a permits response in place of spend, test_clock true, missing/truncated output and launch failure.\n- Additional unknown JSON fields remain compatible. A valid allowed response with warnings advances once and surfaces the warnings to the caller.\n- A test in the style of tests/integrating.rs checks the genuine cases against the binary and runs the reference decoder over all fixtures. Document how a second tool runs its own decoder against the same corpus without Rust or a live target.\n- Scope is the client decision boundary. Existing end-to-end tests keep ownership of the pass engine itself.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Give integrating tools an offline conformance kit for fail-closed decisions"}	blake3:KuDr2ODI7O-yuD__ZDB_XgYCDBxY0LNzPzIORbHAFRQ
1795198148957525	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-mik	{"description":"A spend, status or permits check needs verified totals and the witnessed line. The current read paths read the whole file and Parsed retains entries, adoptions and a hash per line. Long histories therefore consume memory proportional to the number of past spends for each decision.\n\nDelivery\nFactor a streaming JSON Lines verifier in src/ledger.rs with summary and collecting modes. Summary retains running totals, current sequence/tail, the line hash at the witness sequence and one record buffer. Preserve the public read_ledger contract by collecting entries only for callers that request them. Route tally, spend/adopt and summary-only CLI commands to the appropriate mode. Take the witness snapshot at a consistent point in the existing lock order.\n\nAcceptance\n- On legacy, mixed spend/adopt, header-only and extended-witness fixtures, compare totals, receipt sequence, warning states, line hashes and classifications with the existing parser. Do not skip any historical line.\n- Fail closed on a malformed middle line, truncated final line or witnessed prefix mismatch, retaining the relevant diagnostics. Keep parsing existing valid records, including large adoption records.\n- Demonstrate summary memory growth using the same bounded set of counter names and record lengths at 10k and 100k entries. Record the before/after peak memory or instrument retained buffers. Working memory may scale with distinct counters and longest record, independently of line count.\n- Concurrent reader/writer coverage must preserve ledger and witness ordering. Full-history ledger output remains available. This change still scans the file on every decision and promises no constant-time spend.","kind":"task","labels":["ledger"],"size":"m","title":"Verify long ledgers without retaining every entry for a single decision"}	blake3:Pamrmb7JLCoKISjGWOIrCdJJVXtaNDwYMB6wRsPdy5c
1795198245356487	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3k6	{"description":"An owner can translate an approval into a pass, see which declared terms changed, and check representative allowances and refusals offline. A practice run can start independently without erasing any earlier count.\n\nManual exit gate\nReview a pair of valid passes including a hostname-only edit, removal of a names restriction, an overlapping rule and a changed ceiling. Use diff, lint and the owner's case suite on them. Confirm the reported example and the actual policy decision agree. Show the family-specific breadth calculation on a nested-network fixture and document the chosen threshold policy. Run the tested reference-to-ID and practice-run examples. Compare docs/spec.md and docs/PASS-FORMAT.md with the actual output. Close manually when evidence covers each child.","kind":"goal","labels":["goal:manual"],"title":"Review and test the scope an owner approved"}	blake3:LSUoEkwhZitmo7Q-2SFpOnR45aCN8fDxvfmH30d6uIQ
1795198245542004	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1gh	{"description":"Operators need callers to distinguish a recorded spend, a planning answer, a refusal and an indeterminate process or storage error.\n\nManual exit gate\nUse an isolated state directory to exercise one documented CLI and Rust workflow: preflight, count once, then pin the follow-up permission check. Edit the pass between checks and confirm the refusal and the single debit. Repeat the held-lock window transition and the unavailable-witness case with their selected policies. Check the foreign-owner contract and clock-rollback outcome. Run the client conformance corpus, the executable documentation test and the library-only build/tests. Check that the docs distinguish recorded-but-unconfirmed debits from safe retries. Close manually after these results agree with docs/INTEGRATING.md and docs/spec.md.","kind":"goal","labels":["goal:manual"],"title":"Keep live caller decisions tied to the reviewed approval"}	blake3:20rE0ll2yj88OWA4JN7S7dm0-W6cgF77lmn7zXiTSk0
1795198245810705	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-iqo	{"description":"One unit can consume multiple budgets. Its receipt, ledger view and witness must let an operator reconcile the total across processes and revisions.\n\nManual exit gate\nExercise one documented multi-charge spend with two racing clients. Reopen the ledger and compare the receipt, both balances, counter-filtered listing and adoption carry-forward. Read historical single-charge/adoption fixtures and check that an incompatible reader rejects the new line. Review the decision on releasing unused reservations separately from the additive debit model. Run relevant property and CLI tests and reconcile docs/PASS-FORMAT.md, docs/spec.md and the caller example before manual close.","kind":"goal","labels":["goal:manual"],"title":"Account for the full cost of a unit across callers"}	blake3:VrU7zSOkS7pEFHz6i3XMCIKax-IhxbvQxeVAMeRTYm4
1795198245999482	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1oc	{"description":"An operator can recover a lost or replaced ledger with informed consent and answer which approval and ledger prefix a report referred to.\n\nManual exit gate\nIn private temporary directories run the archive/checkpoint/restore exercise and a contested guarded adoption. Verify exact historical bytes, the checkpoint result before and after a new spend, the carried amounts, and the handling of stale previews. Review the 10k/100k summary-memory comparison and full-chain corruption tests. Reconcile README recovery guidance and docs/DESIGN.md with the observed behavior. Run the documented fmt, clippy, test and rustdoc checks on the integrated revision and close manually with the results.","kind":"goal","labels":["goal:manual"],"title":"Recover and independently verify engagement history"}	blake3:6Be1VXiAlRgVYa0Rw494Tc55l3f8JyUsdpcF3YGPl6U
1795198287232431	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-283	{"description":"Define one unambiguous tagged debit carrying a sequence, pass digest, predecessor and bounded list of distinct positive charges. The existing header and ordinary entries stay readable. Implement parsing, tally and display/filter behavior from hand-authored fixture bytes before enabling a writer. Expose one logical entry in the Rust ledger view and a documented JSON shape. Agree on how optional provenance from bn-1du composes with this event.\n\nAccept when old spend/adoption fixtures still pass, a mixed ledger is counted once per debit and once per charged counter, and ledger --counter selects a matching event once. Cover unknown field/version, duplicates, invalid amounts, overflow, truncated record and wrong predecessor. Use a frozen legacy decoder/fixture to demonstrate incompatible readers fail closed. Update docs/PASS-FORMAT.md with canonical examples.","kind":"task","labels":["ledger"],"parent":"bn-iqo","size":"m","title":"Read and reconcile a multi-charge ledger entry"}	blake3:WITkzdNESxO_RmuYSwjqM4hr6rtq_JLSfbZdqicBXTw
1795198287405198	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2yl	{"description":"Wire the completed library transaction into hallpass spend with repeatable named costs. Specify how the existing positional counter/--n form and new form are distinguished. Reject mixed forms, duplicate or empty charges and ambiguous inputs before writing. Leave existing single-counter command and JSON contract usable.\n\nAccept with a subprocess fixture: a two-budget refusal leaves both totals unchanged, a success returns one sequence and the per-counter spent/remaining values, and racing CLI clients cannot split the last units. Reopen with status and ledger --counter, then restore/adopt an older ledger and verify carried totals. Document text/JSON and an executable example using isolated HOME and XDG_STATE_HOME. Clarify which result allows a caller to proceed.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"parent":"bn-iqo","size":"s","title":"Expose atomic charges to CLI callers and exercise the full receipt"}	blake3:Rb9YA5eeeXwtLhWvLCujSVKLil-F_NNfUi203IUmZfc
1795198287573694	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-677	{"description":"Replace the bytes-and-all-hashes parser in src/ledger.rs with one line-by-line verifier. Let consumers collect full events or retain just sequence/tail, totals and requested prefix hashes. Hash original line bytes. Capture the witness sequence under the existing locking protocol, or reread under the same ledger lock. Retain compatibility with valid large adoption lines and old note rules.\n\nAccept using historical fixtures plus proptest on bounded histories. Compare collected events, summary totals, original tail, witnessed prefix (including header) and exact corruption line against the existing contract. Mutate a middle line or final newline and assert both modes reject. Exercise wrong pass ID, extended and rewound witnesses and newly supported record variants whenever present. Integrate through the existing public full-history read path, preserving its result type and behavior. Summary-only command routing and memory measurements belong to bn-mik.","kind":"task","labels":["ledger"],"parent":"bn-1oc","size":"m","title":"Stream and validate ledger lines through one reusable verifier"}	blake3:--31N4fVdzIjCc_BLHrEk7CTIogghMmcAYPesbb_a_Y
1795198287763592	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ss	{"description":"Add an optional reviewed-state precondition to the real adopt operation and the CLI. Under the writer's witness-then-ledger locking protocol re-evaluate the preview inputs including pass digest, ledger location/presence/seq/tail and full witness identity. For a missing ledger, validate the guard while holding the per-ID lock before create_ledger. Preserve the required reason and existing unguarded behavior.\n\nAccept when applying a current preview appends one adoption and produces precisely its projected totals. Replacing the pass, advancing the ledger, changing the witness, or using the wrong pass/path returns a documented stale result before ledger creation, append or witness replacement. Race two applications in separate processes: at most one append. Cover a missing ledger and an existing changed ledger. Document stale and post-append error handling in docs/spec.md and the README recovery example.","kind":"task","labels":["recovery"],"parent":"bn-1oc","size":"m","title":"Apply an adoption only while its preview token still matches"}	blake3:qe0as6tFKUTweuti9ecwf6-2GbHg5aXrEf4-oJbr-TE
1795198287906442	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13u	{"description":"Use tests/integrating.rs or a sibling CLI test to execute the published offline walkthrough. Start with a pass, archive its bytes, spend once and retain a checkpoint separately. Edit the pass, add a spend, verify the checkpoint as extended in another temporary home, and recover the original revision from its digest. Restore the older ledger in the original home and exercise preview then guarded adoption. Assert that verification itself never adopts or mutates local state.\n\nCompare JSON/exit codes with the documented examples and verify the two counters or individual units still reconcile, including carry-forward. State what an independently retained checkpoint establishes and the limits of a user-owned archive. Keep all file operations in temporary directories. This bone owns the cross-feature walkthrough; unit tests stay with their features.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"parent":"bn-1oc","size":"s","title":"Run the closeout and recovery guide against a real engagement fixture"}	blake3:D9n5WZUNGB17BcrGb4JSSnNMEKIlsPCPa1LXFgmHV4c
1795198288112856	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-37i	{"description":"src/ledger.rs can append and sync a debit and then return Error::WitnessNotUpdated with its sequence. docs/spec.md currently describes spend exit 1 as recording nothing. Give a subprocess caller enough information to reconcile the ambiguous outcome before it considers another debit.\n\nSurface the already-known sequence and ledger identity in the JSON error for this case. Distinguish a known durable append from an append/sync failure whose outcome is uncertain. Keep exit 1 and the no-send action for both. In a deterministic test simulate failure between append and witness replacement, reopen the ledger, and assert one debit, matching sequence, and recovery of the witness on the documented path. Verify the client takes no send or automatic retry action. Update the spec exit table, integration guide and affected Rust error docs. An ordinary policy refusal must leave totals unchanged.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"parent":"bn-1gh","size":"s","title":"Expose a recorded debit when updating its witness fails"}	blake3:HNJ-nEUS19tD11BFnbHaf6NrP7MHDaLfwtM2l2nDdBw
1795198398654319	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1cu	{"field":"description","value":"An operator who pinned a pass can tell that the bytes changed, but has to read a TOML diff to decide what to take back to the owner. Give the reviewer a field-level account of the change before they accept a new digest.\n\nDelivery\nAdd a read-only diff OLD NEW command backed by a library comparison of two LoadedPass values. Print both byte digests. Compare approval identity, UTC window, revocation, ceilings and destinations keyed by label. Normalize port ranges and name sets for comparison. List introduced/removed labels and changes of match order explicitly. Treat an ID change as a different approval and ledger identity.\n\nAcceptance\n- A comment edit changes the digest and reports equivalent parsed fields. An equivalent port range and offset spelling produce no scope change.\n- Removing a name restriction, extending the window, restoring a revoked pass or raising a ceiling each gets an explicit before/after row. Mixed and overlapping edits get a review-required classification whenever whole-pass containment is uncertain.\n- An ID change is prominent even when all rules match. Reordering overlapping labels reports its possible effect on the returned label. An invalid input identifies its side and produces no comparison result.\n- Cover text and JSON in tests/cli.rs and document that diff never authorizes a spend. Use src/pass.rs and src/ports.rs. V1 compares declared rules and makes no claim to solve arbitrary policy equivalence.\n\nProperty check: generate small port lists with overlapping intervals and equivalent orderings. Equivalent normalized sets must compare equal. Test 65535 as a range end. Changes to hostname (informational) and names (enforced) must be reported distinctly. Document the diff in docs/spec.md."}	blake3:3Ns0NNzYkr-ZkZAt-4O-7YX28_DeuEIBjx2TSeBUrUY
1795198398919729	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1cu	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-3k6"}	blake3:yVPuoQAWdfaBLF1Pjyrac1U7d1n-Uws_gSh7fdqrrEk
1795198399145884	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2vs	{"field":"description","value":"A reviewer can read a destination's names list and reasonably expect it to constrain the service. Pass::permits_named accepts a query when any matching destination allows it. Another entry can therefore make the apparent restriction ineffective on part of the address and port range.\n\nDelivery\nAdd an advisory lint with a stable finding code, both labels and the overlapping address, protocol and port range. Start with a named rule overlapping a nameless rule. Use Network::overlaps, inclusive port intersection and the existing decision path. Bound or group output on large passes and signal truncation. Expose findings through a read-only CLI operation and JSON.\n\nAcceptance\n- A fixture with two overlapping rules shows exactly which service portion also accepts an unnamed query. Disjoint address families, transports and ports produce no finding.\n- A finding described as an effective permission includes one literal example validated through permits_named at an in-window instant on a non-revoked fixture. Distinguish a structural overlap on a revoked pass from a live permission. Account for IPv4 network edges and explicit host entries.\n- Reordering the rules preserves the finding. Existing check, permits and spend outcomes stay compatible. Add fixture-based tests and a reviewer example in docs/PASS-FORMAT.md.\n- Scope is the interaction between name restrictions and overlapping rules. Aggregate network breadth is already tracked in bn-12w.\n\nUse a generated fixture family with overlapping IPv4 ranges and ports. For every example presented as an effective allowance, replay its unnamed target through the existing matcher during the fixture window. Check a fully disjoint family produces no finding. Group or truncate output deterministically and disclose the limit."}	blake3:Rr4hB85dd6MoOjRMsoWQjR2Oavc-zz0sWKOSemRH6sM
1795198399275993	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2vs	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-3k6"}	blake3:MShYQgk2nHkhdOrjJ7V8XuCrR3O3_eBCA5cnFfrA1hQ
1795198399405754	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-uwj	{"field":"description","value":"An owner can describe the boundaries of an approval in examples that should survive subsequent edits. A mismatched expectation should be visible in review, including when the surprising result is an allowance.\n\nDelivery\nAdd an offline test-pass PASS CASES command and a small versioned case format. Each named case supplies its own offset timestamp, target, optional name and duration, and expected allowance or stable refusal code. Parse through the shipping target/name parsers and evaluate with Pass::permits_named. Use one loaded pass and include its digest in the report.\n\nAcceptance\n- Demonstrate a permitted named service, an unapproved name, and an exclusive window-end case. Editing the pass so the negative example succeeds flags just that case as a mismatch.\n- Report expected and actual outcomes for every valid case, in input order. Define JSON and exits explicitly: 0 all expectations match, 2 mismatches, 1 malformed input or invalid pass. Reject empty suites, duplicate case IDs and unknown fields.\n- Run fixtures with the release binary, no test clock and an empty temporary home. Verify the state directory and ledger were never created.\n- Document a bounded input size/case count and a runnable suite in docs/INTEGRATING.md. This exercises declared policy at supplied instants. Spend previews against live totals remain bn-1na.\n\nKeep suite examples in their own fixture or a dedicated guide linked from docs/INTEGRATING.md. Its existing executable walkthrough expects one TOML pass and runs every console block in order. Extend the harness deliberately if changing that contract. Include explicit at-time and duration equality in the executable cases."}	blake3:DySbNoBeItMx4C-lXy_CCV2KUGg23pCr8mnn0ehV-V0
