1795198401365738	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2rf	{"field":"title","value":"Specify and test read-only decisions when the witness is unavailable"}	blake3:HiKu8hOPSeI5y3rP9jcqgfK6OC9QhRn6AASC6FQ4BQM
1795198401375961	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2rf	{"field":"description","value":"Use the current CLI test an_unusable_state_directory_fails_closed_for_spends as the baseline. Distinguish a healthy fresh state, a missing witness for an existing ledger, an unreadable/unusable witness, and a known missing or divergent ledger. Document whether permits on unavailable evidence issues a warning or fails closed, with a rationale for callers checking an uncounted target.\n\nAccept with a table in docs/spec.md and docs/INTEGRATING.md covering permits, status, ledger and spend. Pin the selected JSON result, exit and warnings for each materially different case. Writes continue to require usable state. Verify a known missing/rewound ledger cannot yield an allowance and read-only checks do not repair files. Keep path-based Rust checks aligned with the CLI when they land."}	blake3:lcbZl1TZMeg8X-iWDwc1dc9bN0B2BxeHmzaFs3PwQqY
1795198401450191	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2rf	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1gh"}	blake3:3ka0XfSgHPqJGQbeJ6Sx9TLfFGGvIxRyU8x1zA5cB_c
1795198401537766	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1my	{"field":"title","value":"Make the CLI version and the documented compatibility contract agree"}	blake3:FSba1k8OmrZB7Qu18v91MemVwuPWXDlwHOMNXcCTiO8
1795198401548146	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1my	{"field":"description","value":"Reconcile the package/Cargo.lock version, --version, and the historical behavior described in docs/DESIGN.md and docs/PASS-FORMAT.md. Document how consumers identify the binary version and how additive JSON fields, stable codes and ledger versions are handled. If exposing the binary version in JSON, use one source of truth and cover both success and usage-error paths.\n\nAccept when --version matches Cargo package metadata, the documented versions line up with actual reader behavior, and tests/integrating.rs still accepts extra fields. A release or publication is separate work."}	blake3:wTRnq3J-rJDdqbAUT4rtNM-vAmPTkOda6ErE20IvM3g
1795198401548633	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1my	{"field":"size","value":"s"}	blake3:ohrDeyWMWTrF9jTQeHcmHW6wa8wnQg58kvWUKWXIutM
1795198401619307	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1my	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1gh"}	blake3:I9y_uXlyTVo7MJ8RZ6T2dgg-stJMl5NIvnsq82YiHK8
1795198401707223	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-210	{"field":"title","value":"Decide and test which Unix file owners may supply a pass"}	blake3:WkXpK_IvKuKo0eY4xK2YV_p0NxK_nf0EW31A50_y0lw
1795198401713108	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-210	{"field":"description","value":"Resolve the shared-directory trust question in src/files.rs. Mode 0644 permits its owner to edit the pass; UID equality would also exclude legitimate centrally managed read-only approvals. Record the intended trust boundary in docs/DESIGN.md with an example of each deployment. If ownership becomes a load requirement, use a portable safe Unix API and report the expected and actual owner. Preserve unsafe_code = forbid.\n\nAccept with tests of the selected owner/mode policy for same-UID private and readable files, a foreign owner, and group/other-writable files. Factor the metadata decision so the normal suite needs no root or chown; test real file loading for the available same-user cases. Keep README and PASS-FORMAT file requirements consistent. The recorded decision determines whether a runtime change is needed."}	blake3:csjkLHTeDd_cYQXZZ1KfdXfXFyxfrd62kDTUpnoJcIs
1795198401713616	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-210	{"field":"size","value":"s"}	blake3:_iTutkPJXglfXS9FFqjlrd_Ag1cWUcw4Wg27oqmueNc
1795198401788843	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-210	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1gh"}	blake3:oShERb-DYH3DTbnHF7D-eARIjQT8Js_DhGYS8wlCrbk
1795198401971378	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-649	{"field":"title","value":"Evaluate the spend window after waiting for the writer locks"}	blake3:Sepo2GymLH4a2-jn0CX31DIVE-IfFwwrW-EGK3r0UV0
1795198401987467	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-649	{"field":"description","value":"A spend currently uses an instant sampled before waiting for the per-ID and ledger locks. Define an additive library path that can sample an injected clock inside the critical section and use it for the ordinary CLI spend. Retain deterministic explicit-time callers and document their caller-supplied instant contract. A timeout alone cannot guarantee a still-open window.\n\nAccept with a coordinated test (barriers or controlled clock, no timing race): request starts inside the window, waits for a lock, then acquires it at valid_until. Refuse and append no debit. Cover the exclusive duration boundary and a successful post-lock call whose recorded time matches the decision. Preserve the lock order and validate the latest pass. Update docs/INTEGRATING.md and docs/DESIGN.md with the effective decision instant."}	blake3:tGdsuUsXWXqrE1rk9-Rrj3YiQPJ4tDdGGeNsEp1ROyg
1795198401988203	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-649	{"field":"size","value":"m"}	blake3:85DOPOd6Od0m-7hI_hVLoJxtp3Nu5JjKKG7qTH8GChY
1795198402085396	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-649	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1gh"}	blake3:E0Ajo7V5sFkD9DW7fSWE0mgYkatsrQL09V41pIDXgBA
1795198402212690	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-13z	{"field":"title","value":"Set a clock-rollback policy for spends with historical ledger times"}	blake3:Rb-D-rqgyk4j3z_GQCqv2gAcNMYh0aTo258o6NE67F8
1795198402222771	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-13z	{"field":"description","value":"Once the live spend samples time under the lock, decide how a verified ledger timestamp affects a later earlier instant. Account for old out-of-order histories, clock correction, and a prior timestamp far in the future. Record the threshold (including equality), response, and a recovery an operator can actually use without resetting counts. If the supported policy is advisory, expose and test its warning explicitly.\n\nAccept with deterministic cases for a normal clock, the threshold just either side, two serialized callers and a future-dated history. Keep legacy histories readable. Show what status and a subsequent spend report, and document the limits of a local clock in docs/DESIGN.md. New ledger events or an approval override for recovery require a separately scoped follow-up if selected."}	blake3:wKhNPnC0rnWpV9zeE4zPZFfJqgRtwa0aE1gL26NBz30
1795198402335859	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-13z	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1gh"}	blake3:0lPQY9pHYle0coDByw7CATI-ikGJaZY2wdYqTemvgDE
1795198402451219	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3bz	{"field":"title","value":"Commit all approved counter charges as one durable library spend"}	blake3:8_tHO7wEvQjbKG82MPVH0TSOLIzkx8vyPLNTl9K2j8Y
1795198402456703	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3bz	{"field":"description","value":"Deliver the Rust transaction for one unit with several known costs using the verified record reader in bn-283. Bound the request at MAX_COUNTERS and reject duplicate counters, empty lists, invalid/zero amounts or overflow. Reuse digest, target/name and window checks against the pass read under the existing locks. Validate every ceiling before appending one record and syncing, then write a witness containing all resulting totals. Preserve single-counter callers and their on-disk representation.\n\nAccept with requests=1 and bytes=100: (1,101) consumes neither budget and (1,100) commits exactly once. A missing counter, stale digest or policy refusal adds no debit. Race requests with overlapping budgets, replay in seq order and compare every balance against a serial reference model. Use proptest for small lists and starting totals. Validate carry-forward after adoption. Simulate post-append failure and reconcile the committed sequence before retry. State the library result and durability boundary in rustdoc. bn-2yl owns the CLI and published workflow."}	blake3:NeXtiIm2zGDtdhGdywQrxJPmK6ZWp6AiP7SOF2TvZ4g
1795198402457673	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3bz	{"field":"size","value":"m"}	blake3:xeAiLrZUmXFHP0uZO7F15KY1oXi-XsO1k9_zIMa-CmA
1795198402552433	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3bz	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-iqo"}	blake3:IgtA96-tqI7YXCaCdk2kLQXtSg-TMK1xLc8csr4Wj5c
1795198402658506	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1du	{"field":"title","value":"Retain the destination and caller reference with an opt-in spend receipt"}	blake3:0AQRFDojpsNMTtzZrAImAuR4ytVyv_jpJ_sz5PTeIyU
1795198402664386	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1du	{"field":"description","value":"Spend presently records a note but drops the matched target. Specify a bounded structured receipt for caller/run reference and, where requested, the literal target, canonical request name and matched label observed under the lock. Use an explicit recording choice for sensitive details. Define how a receipt represents more than one target if multi-target requests are supported. Ordinary historic and single-counter entries stay readable.\n\nAccept with a new writer/reader round trip, an older ledger with a plain note, and a future/unknown field rejected safely by an incompatible reader. Verify ledger filtering and totals across pass edits. Test output escaping and size limits for caller text. Show a caller correlating its own run record with seq and digest in docs/INTEGRATING.md; document privacy and on-disk compatibility in docs/PASS-FORMAT.md. Coordinate optional metadata with the multi-charge reader."}	blake3:TbPCSiH47r7vUlCac51Ln7LT-qEM_d3YLpIVmUv9w1E
1795198402750629	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1du	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-iqo"}	blake3:Xsdhe3TMIFQe-A3csu-MtJxNY50Q9wejoEkETh8C_CY
1795198402861432	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-34a	{"field":"title","value":"Decide whether unused reservations may ever lower a recorded count"}	blake3:2HBimVK8pilDo-SRtkYNKfh-p6W8aC9B67QbdjO0i9A
1795198402867187	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-34a	{"field":"description","value":"The current count is monotone, while the original caller reserves in advance and may want credit for work that never began. This is an owner's accounting decision. Compare the existing first-send workflow, a read-only preflight plus first-send spend, and a reservation/release model. Document what happens after a crash or ambiguous append, who can assert the unit never left, and how adoption and concurrent callers would reconcile totals.\n\nClose with an explicit owner disposition and a worked budget example in docs/DESIGN.md and docs/INTEGRATING.md. If the decision is to support release, create separately estimated implementation and compatibility work with duplicate-release, carry-forward and concurrency coverage before changing accounting. Keep this bone open while the decision is outstanding."}	blake3:zcLyIt01bXYwUyWtgj7K-T1zKFRYe68OxwScDveyezo
1795198402867687	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-34a	{"field":"size","value":"s"}	blake3:lQfuftXCjjSPW8Q-aJdIPL6WZs8NZris-nWOzFZydqo
1795198402966703	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-34a	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-iqo"}	blake3:LaYu2K14MkM3d6nn6ZUr4KufDxqN9-8FIrJeKLyLEcg
1795198403093452	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-mik	{"field":"title","value":"Use streaming summaries for live decisions and measure retained memory"}	blake3:QfvIm4AaZhpxq5FbVajhABwChtsACsiBb5cTiNVM_0g
1795198403103678	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-mik	{"field":"description","value":"Route tally, permits/status and spend/adopt summary paths through bn-677. Full-history public reads and ledger listings still collect events. Preserve writer lock order, sequence accounting, warnings, and moved/extended witness checks. Snapshot the same pass and witness used by the decision; never trust a previously verified prefix as permission to skip lines.\n\nAccept when summary-only callers match the full read on fresh, mixed, header-only and corrupt histories. Add a concurrent reader/writer test and reject a bad interior line even with a matching tail fixture where applicable. Measure or instrument retained memory at 10k and 100k fixed-size events using the same bounded set of counter names. Record before/after method, sizes and results; extra retained memory should depend on distinct counters and largest line, independently of total history length. Document the remaining linear scan cost in docs/DESIGN.md."}	blake3:p8RAQ39OqvqkBvuSNfUxwC29Kr2NHCIQkWp51dAgl3A
1795198403294182	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-mik	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1oc"}	blake3:makBgRNy98qAQVRwYFBSeQXxbOiy9_qMj2TXMd4BgX4
1795198403671414	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-11g	{"field":"description","value":"An owner who keeps a closeout or handoff report needs to check later whether the ledger still contains the exact history they reviewed, even in a separate account with no local witness. The chain already has a compact commitment to that history.\n\nDelivery\nAdd offline checkpoint export and verification for an existing ledger. Export a versioned object with pass ID, sequence and hash of the exact line at that sequence (the header for seq 0). Verify the whole supplied ledger, including later lines, then classify an exact match, a valid extension, a shorter ledger or a different history. Reuse src/ledger.rs parsing and byte-hashing conventions.\n\nAcceptance\n- Checkpoint after two spends, copy it to a separate temporary directory, append another spend and verify the copy as a valid extension with one later line. The original two-entry file verifies as exact.\n- Edited or missing checkpointed history, a malformed suffix, a different pass ID and a missing ledger cannot verify. A header-only ledger has a defined checkpoint. A pass edit keeping its ID does not by itself change recorded history.\n- Export fails on unusable or conflicting local evidence and states when local corroboration is absent. Standalone verification reports its supplied checkpoint as the trust input and never creates or adopts a witness.\n- Document text/JSON outcomes and external retention. A checkpoint copied from the same rewritten files cannot establish independent provenance. Omit notes, paths and owner contact details from the object.\n\nUse the sequence-selective hashing from bn-677 to check a supplied prefix while validating the entire ledger, including a corrupt suffix. Define machine outcomes: exact/extended are successes, a well-formed but contradictory checkpoint is a mismatch, malformed inputs or unavailable ledger are errors. Document the exit mapping. Expose missing local corroboration explicitly when exporting and allow standalone verification in a fresh home."}	blake3:k71Tzu9KJO9QNpnq9JDJ9dlo8HOuqKEjZ14zUw9zyDE
1795198403791174	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-11g	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1oc"}	blake3:RvZtCxAI_zEq7J1ImBwRRjHaTDYPezIdbOyZpQd-dE4
1795198403976338	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3w0	{"field":"description","value":"A historical spend names a SHA-256 digest. After a pass is edited, the ledger alone cannot show an owner which window, ceilings and names that digest represented. Allow explicit retention of the approved revision at review time.\n\nDelivery\nAdd an opt-in archive command taking a pass and an explicit private directory. Read one bounded byte buffer through src/files.rs, validate and hash that same buffer, then publish it atomically as a private file named from the digest. Add read-only lookup by full digest that verifies the stored bytes before returning the revision. Retrieval is for review and cannot update the live pass or its accounting state.\n\nAcceptance\n- Archive A, record a spend, edit only a comment and archive B. Looking up the spend digest returns A byte for byte including comments. Looking up B returns its exact bytes.\n- Archiving A twice or concurrently is idempotent. A conflicting pre-existing file, symlink, insecure directory, invalid pass, or damaged lookup result gives an error without overwriting anything. Missing digests are explicit.\n- Test a revision change during archive with either one self-consistent snapshot or an error. Ordinary spends never depend on the archive. Output never quietly materializes a recovered pass beside a live ledger.\n- Document local retention of sensitive approval text and show retrieval using a digest from ledger JSON. V1 uses an explicit local directory and needs neither automatic history capture nor a remote store.\n\nCover absent archive, wrong digest, two concurrent creators, and bytes that change during input reading using temporary private directories. Specify the path and file mode trust checks for both store and retrieval. Document raw-byte export separately from JSON metadata so machine consumers can reconstruct the original bytes exactly."}	blake3:WGiLSLqp10xM9EmSmVxtc40f7eXlDRLhGsgSqpqd_Vs
1795198404101797	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3w0	{"field":"parent","value":"bn-1oc"}	blake3:__2WqpB0E9xBUe3rETJNTbZZVEEstOv9eq53QeAniKI
1795198404223509	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-k5s	{"field":"title","value":"Preview adoption with the exact totals a recovery would carry forward"}	blake3:9jqYhG5LfsfyBvLWVEbOuE-AxyFPiGwJYnHnyaTsK5c
1795198404233863	codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-k5s	{"field":"description","value":"Extract the per-counter carry calculation from adopt and use it in a side-effect-free Rust inspection and CLI adopt --preview. Report the case, pass/ledger identity, prior witness, sequences, carried delta and projected totals. For adoptable states return a versioned token covering the exact decision inputs, including ledger presence/path and witness contents, for guarded apply in bn-2ss.\n\nAccept when six witnessed and four surviving calls preview as four present, two carried and six resulting. Check missing ledger, missing witness, matching/extended history, corrupt ledger, incompatible ID and unreadable witness. Only adoptable snapshots can yield a token. Preserve valid existing zero-line states. Verify neither healthy nor failed previews create any directories, locks, ledger headers or witnesses. Test the carry calculation against componentwise maxima and the ordinary adopt result on independent copies. Document snapshot semantics, JSON and exit behavior in docs/spec.md."}	blake3:aIi63eNBL4eDLWczCpQZqXvivg84Sj6aph85JX32jog
