1795209158102838	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2au	{"description":"A client asks to debit one request and 100 bytes for two named endpoints. The current reference decoder in docs/conformance/check.py compares pass_id, digest, counter and n. Charge-only replies lack the scalar pair, and in scalar mode a reply for a different endpoint can satisfy the example check.\n\nDeliver a versioned extension to docs/conformance/cases.json and the reference decoder. Represent the expected request explicitly with its mode, ordered canonical targets and names, charge pairs and optional caller reference. Check the reply against everything it echoes, including agreement between scalar and array fields when both are present. Document canonical input construction and which request fields are observable. Keep the existing scalar corpus runnable.\n\nAcceptance\n- Exercise the genuine CLI with separate scalar, positional-with-receipt and charge-only successes, including two targets and a reference. Each matching production reply advances a callback spy exactly once.\n- Change only the second target, its name, one charge, the reference, or an overlapping scalar field. Each mismatch stops the callback. Cover missing/extra array entries, boolean amounts, a preview and a recorded-but-unconfirmed debit.\n- Additional unrelated JSON fields remain compatible. Run the offline corpus and tests/integrating.rs with temporary local state. Document how another language can run the same cases.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Bind client acceptance to every endpoint and charge in a spend reply"}	blake3:UtPmhK_muSTU9Xi2Gabyk8RKWDRB1rINnntC030Eayk
1795209158242612	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2i8	{"description":"After a timeout an operator has a pass ID and a caller reference. Today the recovery guide asks them to inspect ledger output manually. The same reference may appear twice, and adoption can preserve totals while losing the original receipts.\n\nDeliver a read-only receipt lookup by exact caller reference, scoped to one pass. Return the match count, sequence, digest, charges and any retained target evidence. Verify the full chain and compare the witness before releasing results. Reuse src/ledger.rs and src/ledger/debit.rs and retain at most a bounded number of matching rows. Surface truncation, witness status, and adoption history. Describe an absent result as absence from the available ledger.\n\nAcceptance\n- One reference after a lost response identifies the recorded sequence and charges even after a later pass edit. Repeated references report ambiguity and all matches up to the bound. A legacy scalar row or reference-free debit supplies no reference.\n- A restored ledger with carried totals explicitly reports the history limit. A damaged suffix after a matching row fails the lookup. Test a missing witnessed ledger and a fresh pass separately.\n- Provide a worked reconciliation using the caller's own durable run record. Every result is historical evidence. No result authorizes sending, retries a debit, or refunds budget. Verify ledger and witness bytes are unchanged.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Find the recorded debit after a caller loses its spend response"}	blake3:f5mYK350RH4BmqJ8jbxAET0sPRVuSdA0C4MvBVhxiR8
1795209158396886	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2xv	{"description":"A digest lets an operator detect an edited pass. An owner and operator with an existing SSH signing relationship also need a way to establish who endorsed the original digest. Offer a CLI-only, offline verify-approval workflow backed by OpenSSH detached signatures and an independently supplied allowed-signers file.\n\nTake the expected signer identity from the verifier's invocation and use a fixed Hallpass signature namespace. Reuse the safe exact-byte read used by archive_pass. Validate, hash and feed the same byte buffer to ssh-keygen -Y verify via stdin with separate argv values. On success return the verified principal, pass ID and digest for permits/spend --digest. The operator owns the mapping between principal and owner. Document the owner's signing step and how the verifier provisions the trust file independently.\n\nAcceptance\n- A temporary signer and test trust file verify a fixture. Changing one comment, changing the principal, using another namespace, or supplying an untrusted key fails verification. A missing verifier gives an actionable error.\n- Editing the pass after verification produces pass-changed on a subsequent digest-pinned live request. An expired signed pass can verify historically and still refuses live use.\n- Text and JSON distinguish signature verification from live authorization. Ordinary CLI and library use keep their existing dependencies. Tests use disposable keys and private temp directories.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Verify an owner signature and pin subsequent decisions to the signed bytes"}	blake3:u5iEEsv7eajRRogsF44SIUZGzJAkBzOtjPoQWT1d69g
1795209158549795	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2hg	{"description":"An owner can read the field diff and still miss that removing one name restriction admits an unnamed request, or that reordering overlapping destinations changes only the label recorded on a receipt. Give the reviewer an actual request they can replay for each difference found.\n\nAdd an opt-in examples mode to diff in src/review.rs. V1 covers destination, port, name and order edits at an explicit review instant. Derive a deterministic, bounded set of boundary candidates from edited rules on both sides, including unnamed queries, then run the real permits_named evaluator against both passes. Return the target, name, exact instant, before/after result and matched label or refusal code. Show the declared field diff alongside the examples and mark the search as sampled.\n\nAcceptance\n- An expanded port range yields a refused-to-allowed example. Removing a listed name yields an unnamed example. Reordering overlapping entries can yield an attribution-only example. A comment edit yields none.\n- Replay every emitted example independently against both fixtures. Include an IPv4 edge, an IPv6 range and an inactive window. Cap candidate work as well as printed rows and report when a cap is reached.\n- Empty or truncated samples never certify equivalence or clear the existing review_required flag. Examples are local evaluations and perform no DNS or network I/O.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Show concrete decisions that change between approval revisions"}	blake3:7dyjGSWvyESwEnzF5ziq0tElDbBub5eOfQ066NMfhRw
1795209158704077	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1x5	{"description":"A reviewer has the ledger and an archive of exact pass revisions. Today they must correlate each receipt's digest, timestamp, names and labels by hand. Add a read-only audit command that answers whether each retained endpoint was in scope at the instant recorded on that debit.\n\nUse retrieve_pass in src/archive.rs, Pass::parse, the verified ledger reader, and permits_named at the stored instant with duration zero. Check archived pass ID and digest. Compare each ordered target's decision and matched label with the receipt. Group missing evidence by reason and list affected sequences. Bound retained details and parsed-revision cache. Report the witness status independently of scope replay.\n\nAcceptance\n- Two debits on opposite sides of an approval edit each use their own archived revision even if the current pass is revoked. Detect a test receipt with a valid chain but a mismatched recorded label.\n- Distinguish corroborated endpoints, policy mismatches and insufficient evidence. Legacy spends, receipts with no targets and adoption carry do not count as verified endpoints. Missing archives produce an incomplete audit. Corrupt ledgers or digest-invalid archive entries fail the command.\n- Document and test machine outcomes for complete, incomplete, mismatch and input error, including a mix of incomplete evidence and a mismatch. The report limits its claim to recorded endpoint scope at the stated instant. Original requested duration, actual traffic and unrecorded endpoints are outside that evidence.","kind":"task","labels":["audit"],"size":"m","title":"Replay recorded destination receipts against their archived approval"}	blake3:9kM8G6hkqqLKtu82lIyT_1Ob0oFRYhOWDwenU3qvNmM
1795209158853299	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3s2	{"description":"Atomic debits protect all supplied counters. A pass with requests and bytes ceilings still accepts a caller that charges requests alone. Give an owner a compact way to require a positive charge for each named budget on every committed unit.\n\nAdd an opt-in, versioned approval declaration containing a bounded list of required counter names. Validate uniqueness and membership in ceilings. Choose the smallest encoding that existing strict readers reliably reject when they encounter this rule, and retain format-1 behavior for existing files. Put the check in shared policy used by scalar and multi-charge Rust APIs, CLI spends and previews. Apply it to targetless spends too. Keep permits a destination/window query. Document that correctness of caller-reported quantities still belongs to the caller.\n\nAcceptance\n- For a pass requiring requests and bytes, requests=1 alone returns a specific refusal identifying bytes. Supplying both succeeds atomically. A targetless debit, positional scalar form or dry run cannot omit a required counter.\n- Preserve the current digest/window/destination refusal precedence. A missing required charge changes no balances or event count. Preview and commit agree on a stable snapshot. Old ledger entries remain readable.\n- Include a legacy approval that still permits one counter, an old-reader rejection fixture for the new declaration, duplicate and undeclared names, and surfacing of the new rule in status and approval diff.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Let an owner require specific counters on every debit"}	blake3:VqZbsXDhVQhDUtGVg9IapavDNaGwHmEXo9RhpYrXh7M
1795209159012191	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1wk	{"description":"A packet filter can restrict an IP and port. It cannot inspect the application name or account for a request by itself. An operator handing a pass to another tool should be able to see exactly which restrictions still require a cooperating component.\n\nAdd a pure compatibility report plus a CLI wrapper accepting an explicit, small capability declaration. Cover literal endpoint checks, application names, live window/revocation checks and named counters charged. Compare against the validated pass and return one finding per unmet requirement, identifying the destination label or counter. Model this as a check for coverage of the entire pass by the declared consumer. The informational hostname field creates no name requirement. Treat the capability declaration as supplied by the operator or integrator.\n\nAcceptance\n- A packet-only profile on a named HTTPS pass with a requests ceiling reports the label and the missing counter. The same profile with time checks covers an unnamed, unmetered literal-only fixture.\n- A tool that charges calls but not bytes reports the exact missing budget. Unknown capabilities or malformed declarations produce a usage error. Report the pass digest with stable result codes for covered, gaps and invalid input.\n- Include a tested example in docs/INTEGRATING.md. Explain what a cooperating second component must cover. A fully covered report describes declared features and never returns a live permission or writes accounting state.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Show which approval restrictions a consuming tool can honor"}	blake3:oOqSoVEYvNBrtur-aOwpm5Kw7aPG1N_6L5M9ZydBmc4
1795209159165534	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3d8	{"description":"Three steps cost 40 bytes apiece and 100 remain. Three independent dry runs each appear to fit. A planner needs to discover before starting that step three exceeds the shared budget.\n\nAdd a bounded, versioned local scenario file with ordered step IDs, explicit instants, optional duration, targets and charge lists. Load one validated pass and one witness-checked accounting snapshot. Reuse the pure debit decision in src/ledger/debit.rs with an in-memory tally. Apply each accepted step's full cost to projected balances. Stop at the first refused step and mark remaining steps unevaluated. Return the source digest, starting balances and per-step evidence. Clearly identify simulation output so live client decoders stop.\n\nAcceptance\n- With 100 bytes available, 40/40/40 projects 20 remaining after step two and flags step three. A failed multi-charge step changes none of its projected balances. A later step at the exclusive expiry boundary is refused.\n- Compare a small mixed scenario to successive actual debits in a temporary practice directory at the same instants. Seed the snapshot with historical spends and adoption carry. A damaged ledger fails before the first projection.\n- Bound input bytes, steps and targets, enforce nondecreasing instants, and check arithmetic. A fresh simulation creates no ledger or witness. Existing files remain byte-identical. A later live spend still checks current state.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Rehearse a sequence of planned debits against one budget snapshot"}	blake3:iRAwqdjbPWxz1ymnOhS6EsVfdzxmzsZXU9NaHe5V52I
1795209159315676	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-zw1	{"description":"After a move or restore, an operator may have several files claiming to be the engagement ledger. Adopting the first copy can discard available receipts. Use the existing witness to identify which backups preserve the independently remembered prefix before the operator moves any files.\n\nProvide a read-only comparison of a bounded list of explicit candidate paths against one captured local witness. Construct the checkpoint from its pass ID, sequence and tail, then reuse the full-file verification in src/ledger.rs. Report each file's relationship to the anchor, its verified tip and per-file errors. Group identical tips. If multiple tips extend the anchor, report ambiguity unless their prefix relationship is actually verified. The longest file alone is insufficient evidence.\n\nAcceptance\n- Include an exact candidate, a valid extension, an older file, a foreign ID, and a matching prefix followed by a corrupt suffix. Keep input ordering and distinguish damaged evidence from a valid mismatch.\n- Two independent branches that both extend the witness must both be reported without selecting an automatic winner. Two identical files can be recognized as copies. An unavailable witness yields a clear inconclusive result.\n- Exercise this when the live ledger is missing. Show the captured witness identity and explain rechecking after restoration. No directory crawl, copy, rename, adoption or witness update is part of the comparison.","kind":"task","labels":["recovery"],"size":"m","title":"Identify ledger backups that preserve the last witnessed history"}	blake3:Sb344lyHt_m6Gkm1msOCzQPGgdN6pltnsvHW6ulXSnY
1795209159469424	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1bu	{"description":"A caller with a latency budget currently has to wait indefinitely for flock or kill the process and reconcile an uncertain debit. Provide an opt-in lock-wait deadline that can report when acquisition failed before the debit could start.\n\nThread a monotonic wait budget through the witness lock and the subsequent exclusive ledger lock in the real scalar and multi-charge spend paths. Use the existing lock order and one shared deadline across both waits. Define zero as a single nonblocking attempt per lock and omission as today's blocking behavior. Expose the option in Rust and the CLI. Keep the live wall-clock policy sample after acquisition and preparation. Scope the limit to lock acquisition so a slow fsync retains its actual recorded/uncertain semantics.\n\nAcceptance\n- With either lock held by a test process, a bounded waiter returns a distinct exit-1 lock-timeout error and no debit. A preparatory file or empty header may already exist. Test witness-lock release when acquisition of the ledger lock times out.\n- Coordinate contention tests with process barriers. Exercise a zero wait, release inside the budget, and a window that expires while the waiter is queued. Only a real committed debit returns an allowed sequence.\n- Confirm default behavior through the existing concurrent spend tests, and document the distinction between lock timeout, external cancellation, append uncertainty and a post-append witness failure.","kind":"task","labels":["ledger"],"size":"m","title":"Give spend callers a lock-wait limit with a definite pre-append outcome"}	blake3:WtLyNxGAKVqtfFE72RK0mkjwCSI9xN6ktGXUhtNHKhk
1795211567651243	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ft9	{"description":"Exercise guarded adoption with a real restored ledger and an independent local witness from another valid history. Use at least one equal counter, one higher on each side, and a counter omitted from one side. Include a lowered ceiling. Verify the preview, exact positive carry, returned balances and a fresh ledger replay against independently specified expectations. Confirm an adopted over-limit counter refuses new spending. Isolate both histories and state directories inside temporary directories.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting","verification"],"size":"s","title":"Check restored balances against the maximum for each counter"}	blake3:FWO7u-EaHrc0XOo1h3-JlNIAIJp8XMEuEt-3H1qgpZ4
1795211567808095	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1sj	{"description":"Extend the current single-decision property with shrinkable sequences using the existing proptest dependency and public Rust accounting APIs. Mix scalar and multi-counter debits, read-only previews, refusals, ceiling edits, saved snapshots and guarded restores. Track expected balances, spend events and adoption sequences independently of the ledger implementation and compare after each step. Verify projected balances on previews and all-or-nothing charging. Include a deterministic history that reaches the interactions even when the random sample is small. Keep it runnable under normal cargo test with isolated state.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting","verification"],"size":"m","title":"Check mixed accounting histories against an independent balance model"}	blake3:8-UdR3rCTl2F4gluK5OUulQMkgwPD7NLYm0FxuKwLsg
