1795381521188958	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3uf	{"description":"An owner can require requests and bytes on every debit today, but an integration that forgets --to can still debit both without any destination check. Add an optional require_target_v1 = true approval term. Existing strict readers should reject a pass that declares it. Absence preserves targetless accounting. This term requires a scope decision only. Target retention continues to require the caller's explicit opt-in.\n\nUse the shared debit policy in src/ledger.rs and src/ledger/debit.rs after digest and window checks, before checking charges. Cover Rust scalar and multi-charge callers, CLI, previews and rehearsal. Surface the term in check, status, diff and consumer-coverage so a caller can declare whether it supplies targets on every debit.\n\nAcceptance\n- Against the same pass and clock, a targetless positional debit, an atomic charge list and their previews all return target-required, exit 2 in the CLI, with no new sequence or balance change. A targetless rehearsal stops on that step.\n- An in-scope target succeeds without being retained. An out-of-scope second target refuses the whole unit with index 2. An expired or digest-mismatched pass keeps its existing first refusal.\n- Run a legacy targetless approval fixture, verify malformed declaration handling and show an older reader rejects the new key. Add the authoring example to docs/PASS-FORMAT.md and the JSON workflow to spend help.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Require a checked destination on every debit when the owner requests it"}	blake3:8UASTvSQD7nITY71k-RgMZz_enoQHlbdDHVg22YoQbA
1795381521423417	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2bn	{"description":"An operator who verifies an owner's signature and then archives the pass opens the pathname twice. An edit between those commands can leave an archive containing a different revision. Add verify-approval --archive DIR so one successful invocation gives the operator both a digest to pin and a retrievable copy of the signed bytes.\n\nsrc/cli_verify.rs already has the validated byte buffer sent to ssh-keygen. Extract the publication portion of src/archive.rs so verification can publish that same buffer to the existing private content-addressed archive after the signature succeeds. Keep the trust file and expected principal external. This operation retains pass bytes only.\n\nAcceptance\n- In tests/verify_approval.rs, deterministically replace the source pathname after the verifier receives its input. Retrieve the returned digest and compare exact bytes, including the original line ending.\n- Invalid signature or wrong principal publishes nothing and exits 2. An insecure directory, bad existing entry or publication failure exits 1. Successful repeat verification reuses an identical entry. Exercise the existing verify-only and archive-only paths.\n- JSON success identifies digest, principal and requested archive destination. Provide a help example that verifies and retains, pins a live check and retrieves the original after a comment edit. Document what must be kept separately to reverify the signature.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Archive the exact approval bytes that passed signature verification"}	blake3:H6iLCG2qXeCH5kaBMLC3tDQXfSzqQCYUeRNJ22vy4JU
1795381522697188	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2pu	{"description":"Two workers can submit the same durable unit reference before either sees the other's response. receipt finds duplicates after both charges have counted. Add opt-in spend --require-new-ref with --caller-ref and an equivalent Rust debit option. A duplicate yields a historical sequence for reconciliation and never a fresh allowed reply.\n\nDuring the existing verified writer scan in src/ledger.rs, match the exact reference across all digests of this pass ID while holding both writer locks. Reuse the receipt matcher, bound retained sequence details and avoid a new index or state file. Scope the guarantee to cooperating guarded submissions on this local history. Unguarded submissions keep correlation-only semantics.\n\nAcceptance\n- Two simultaneous guarded processes with ample budget and the same reference produce one debit. Same reference with changed charges, targets or digest pin for a new revision cannot create another debit. Preserve ordinary digest/window refusal precedence and test distinct references.\n- On a known match return exit 2, duplicate-reference and bounded historical evidence. On an absent reference after adoption, or in an existing nonempty unanchored history, return inconclusive without appending. Allow first use on a fresh pass. Verify to the tip before releasing a result, including after a lost witness update.\n- Reject a missing caller reference. A preview can show an observed match but reserves nothing. Add a concurrent CLI test, a consumer fixture that never treats the duplicate response as permission to send, and a lost-reply example in docs/EVIDENCE.md. External execution still needs the caller's own record.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Refuse a second debit for the same caller reference under the writer locks"}	blake3:ItBmGCy9dwIm4PbK81X1kNpL3DLogq41Ca_vzFCcbak
1795381523111029	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bqp	{"description":"An endpoint audit can corroborate every retained destination while saying nothing about whether each debit fit the owner's budget. Add a read-only audit-charges PASS ARCHIVE for the numerical and mandatory-counter terms, including scalar spends without receipts.\n\nStream verified events in sequence order via src/ledger/evidence.rs. Track cumulative per-counter spend across digest changes. For each debit, use its archived digest and pass ID, the pre-debit totals and the shared required-charge and ceiling rules. Apply all carried amounts at the adoption sequence. Missing revisions leave a gap at their events but the recorded arithmetic still advances. Report the witness separately.\n\nAcceptance\n- Set an old ceiling to 10, spend 8, lower the ceiling to 5, then restore it to 12 and charge 4. Earlier charges are judged by their own revisions. Test a structurally valid historical overcharge, an unknown counter and a missing mandatory charge with the offending sequence and counter.\n- Cover scalar and atomic debits, carry-forward, a missing archive preceding a checkable debit, and a corrupt suffix. Distinguish confirmed mismatch, incomplete history, empty history and input error. Carry is never reported as a verified original debit.\n- Bound detail samples and parsed revision cache. Provide text and JSON examples in help and docs/EVIDENCE.md including mixed gaps and mismatches. Keep the accounting verdict independent of endpoint retention and actual work performed.","kind":"task","labels":["audit"],"size":"m","title":"Audit each historical charge against the ceiling in force when it counted"}	blake3:NNyy2BtGEi8ktlcuq3AIrCgbErd4GdCabZvarXGguFw
1795381523298448	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1os	{"description":"A reviewer sees calls change from 10 to 6 in diff, but needs to know eight calls have already counted. Extend diff CURRENT PROPOSED with an opt-in budget impact view. Use the verified live summary for CURRENT once and apply both declarations to the same recorded totals. Read PROPOSED as an approval file only, even when there is a ledger beside it.\n\nShow old and proposed ceilings, spent, remaining, and any amount already over the proposed ceiling per counter. List newly required and removed counters, including previously spent counters that disappear. Bind the projection to both digests and the available witness/tip information. The review is a snapshot and never installs an amendment.\n\nAcceptance\n- Spending 8 of 10 then proposing 6 gives 0 remaining and 2 already over. Proposing exactly 8, raising to 12, adding a fresh counter or removing a spent one each gets a distinct actionable row. Reintroduced counter names retain historical totals, including adoption carry.\n- A different pass ID makes carry-over inapplicable. A fresh same-ID pass shows zero spent. Damaged or divergent current history fails the impact view. Preserve ordinary file-only diff when the option is absent.\n- Exercise both text and JSON in tests/consumer_review.rs with a decoy proposed ledger and verify no accounting files change. Add a help example and document the result and exit semantics for a valid proposal already below spend.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Preview the remaining budget under a proposed approval amendment"}	blake3:ElkUESOLGyFY8n6esGIyyy02IXK1q3oo3GJS8DlEufA
1795381523547720	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-wur	{"description":"An operator closing a run currently makes one receipt query per reference and hand-compares each result. Add reconcile PASS MANIFEST for a bounded, versioned local manifest of expected units. Each unit supplies an exact distinct caller reference, reviewed digest, positive charge pairs and optionally an ordered list of canonical named targets. Require the manifest pass ID to match.\n\nBuild a map of requested references and visit the verified ledger once using src/ledger/evidence.rs. Compare charge sets by counter and targets by position. If endpoints are expected but evidence is absent, mark that row incomplete. Report manifest order, candidate counts and matching sequences, with bounded detail. Other callers may share this ledger.\n\nAcceptance\n- A single fixture contains an exact match, missing reference, duplicate reference, different charge and swapped second endpoint. Exercise a matching reference from another digest, a reference-free debit and adoption after lost receipts. Absent means absent from available history.\n- Return 0 only when all requested rows match, 2 for valid input with discrepancies, 1 on malformed input or failed history verification including a corrupt suffix. Set explicit byte and row limits and test the boundaries. Reuse the existing receipt command's witness handling.\n- Add an example manifest and one real CLI walkthrough to docs/EVIDENCE.md and help. Report accounting reconciliation only. Never infer an external action, retry or refund from any row.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"m","title":"Reconcile a caller's expected debits against the ledger in one pass"}	blake3:qvD5mOl1h3vaEPPvqi_ZC5ETKjoBDgfbDr4SOmviW28
1795381523792946	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-15m	{"description":"If Friday and Saturday both extend the local witness, compare-backups currently returns ambiguous even when Saturday contains Friday byte for byte through Friday's tip. Extend its opt-in ancestry analysis to prove which witnessed extensions are prefixes of other candidates and identify the maximal verified tips.\n\nReuse verified sequence/hash pairs in src/ledger.rs and candidate grouping in src/ledger/evidence.rs. With the existing cap of 32 paths, collect intermediate hashes only at candidate tip sequences. On a second scan verify the same candidate identity and full tip as the first scan or mark it changed. Preserve each candidate's relationship to the single captured witness.\n\nAcceptance\n- For witness 2 and nested tips 4, 6, 8, report one maximal extension at 8 and show the prefix links. Ties with the same hash form one group. Divergent tips of equal or unequal length remain separate, even when both contain a common prefix.\n- A valid prefix followed by a corrupt suffix supplies no trusted branch. Input error, a changed candidate or a missing witness keeps its explicit partial-error/inconclusive outcome. No length-only decision, writes or automatic restoration.\n- Add CLI cases in tests/evidence, document the opt-in result and old default in help and docs/EVIDENCE.md, and retain the existing input order and partial results. Retained memory should scale with the bounded candidate count.","kind":"task","labels":["recovery"],"size":"m","title":"Distinguish successive ledger backups from competing recovery branches"}	blake3:D_f3SgG3eOnD0k3IS7I6ZXbYJtgKBLsRmpTbf4Jw4U8
1795381524072444	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2hu	{"description":"A caller with both request and byte limits wants to know how much of a fixed workload fits. Rehearsal currently requires an explicit step per unit. Add a read-only capacity query accepting one unit in the existing spend syntax, including all charges, targets, duration and optional digest pin. Describe a budget capacity at the decision instant, independent of scheduling or concurrency.\n\nValidate against the shared debit policy and a single verified preview snapshot, including the owner-required target term. For each positive unit cost compute floor(remaining / cost) with integer arithmetic and report the minimum plus all tied limiting counters. Avoid iterating once per possible unit. Preserve policy refusal details even when a charge cannot fit once.\n\nAcceptance\n- With 10 requests and 100 bytes remaining, units priced at 1 request and 24 bytes yield 4, limited by bytes, leaving 4 bytes. Cover ties, exactly one, zero, historical spend above a revised ceiling and the largest supported values.\n- Unknown/missing counters, target-required, a refused second target, expiration and digest mismatch cannot produce a usable capacity. Distinguish a valid unit with zero budget capacity from invalid scope or missing charges. Define and test JSON and exit statuses for these cases.\n- Compare small results with repeated rehearsal steps under a fixed clock. A fresh query creates no ledger or witness. Document the cost example and explain that concurrent debits or expiry can reduce the count before execution.","kind":"task","labels":["accounting"],"size":"s","title":"Show how many identical units fit and which budgets run out first"}	blake3:vLwtHM5h_2560SjafE_lgSYUpvjgWxin0DMdH5ljtWY
1795381524274770	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24j	{"description":"A passing test-pass suite may keep passing after someone removes the name restriction it was meant to protect. Add an opt-in suite-strength check. Evaluate the owner's unmodified suite against the real pass first, then deterministically replay it against bounded in-memory copies with one plausible restriction loosened per copy.\n\nUse the parser and evaluator in src/review.rs. Start with removing a destination's required names and adding an adjacent valid port to a range. For each mutation report the affected label, exact changed term and the first case that distinguishes it. For a survivor, report that the suite did not distinguish the edit. Overlaps can make a declaration edit semantically redundant. Keep all working copies in memory.\n\nAcceptance\n- A named-allowance-only suite misses removal of names. Add a wrong-name refusal and rerun to identify the case that catches the edit. Exercise an adjacent-port refusal, shadowed rules, port endpoints 1 and 65535 and a failing baseline suite.\n- Cap mutation count and total evaluations, expose counts and truncation, and distinguish a complete pass, surviving mutations, a failing baseline and partial analysis in JSON. An incomplete check cannot be reported as full coverage.\n- Test deterministic output, unchanged pass bytes and ordinary test-pass behavior. Include a before/after fixture and example in docs/INTEGRATING.md and help. A survivor is a review prompt, never an instruction to alter the approval.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Show which loosened approval rules the owner's regression suite would miss"}	blake3:AaNwf1_KuQYF7JC8gSxgt9XqY1_NpVSHVAStulvWsvk
1795381524451526	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ua	{"description":"An owner reviewing a tighter approval needs a stronger answer than a clean sample. Add a conservative endpoint-containment check to diff for two revisions of the same pass at an explicit instant when both windows are open. Limit the claim to zero-duration endpoint admission at that instant. Separate changed label attribution, windows and accounting terms remain visible in ordinary diff.\n\nUse a sufficient proof with bounded work. For each proposed destination, find one prior rule covering its network, protocol, normalized port set and name domain. Separately account for the global IPv4 network/broadcast exclusion and explicit-host exception in src/decision.rs. A simpler first version can require equality of the effective blocked-edge address sets. Unsupported unions, budget exhaustion or unproved edge relationships yield inconclusive. If reporting expansion, replay a concrete target through both ordinary evaluators.\n\nAcceptance\n- Certify a narrower port range or a subset of allowed names. A formerly blocked IPv4 edge made usable by a new explicit host can never receive a containment certificate. Include overlapping named and nameless rules, /31, /32 and IPv6 fixtures.\n- Use a small exhaustive model over generated passes, addresses, ports and optional names to check that every certified result has zero new allowances. Test label-only changes, distinct IDs, disjoint windows, and truncation. An empty sample never counts as proof.\n- JSON and text distinguish contained, expanded with replayable example, and inconclusive with reason. Document the precise claim, work limit and exit codes in diff help and docs/spec.md. No new pass format or policy evaluator.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Prove when a proposed revision only narrows endpoint scope"}	blake3:AeQXty7uiktz3ZjAS6XHHwlbXC8XFRCZPV3sEFbnk-4
