1795398055214358	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3hs	{"description":"Sol saw status report a matching witness, then checkpoint --json warn that it “may lag.” In src/ledger.rs, export_checkpoint already compares the witness under the ledger lock but returns only its presence. In src/main.rs the JSON warning treats every present witness alike.\n\nReport the observed relation from that same export snapshot. A matching tip gets no lag warning. For a verified older prefix, identify the witnessed and exported sequences. For a missing witness, report its absence. Preserve the existing local_corroboration field, portable checkpoint payload, and failure on contradictory history. Cover matching, lagging, absent and contradictory witnesses through the CLI, including round-trip use of the exported payload with verify-checkpoint.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Report whether the local witness matches the exported checkpoint"}	blake3:REpdmuNgSfHarhmo4fr2XGg1ZxZX05XODj9bflMeHJM
1795398055374096	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ac	{"description":"Sol had to write a wrapper before performing even one named, two-counter unit of work. Provide a runnable offline caller with a local file-writing callback. Persist the caller reference and intended pinned approval, target and charges before spend. Request a retained receipt and duplicate-reference protection. Reuse docs/conformance/check.py's reply checks to gate the callback.\n\nGive the next operator a caller-side record that distinguishes a refusal, a verified debit, an observed callback completion and an unknown outcome. Demonstrate a normal completion, a refusal, a duplicate and an interruption between debit and confirmed completion. The interruption must be reconcilable by reference without automatically repeating the callback. Assert callback counts and durable journal outcomes in the example tests. The Hallpass ledger remains the authority for debits and the caller supplies the external-work evidence.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"m","title":"Ship a runnable reference caller that journals intent and gates dispatch"}	blake3:h_cddYzWcwil8YKcsMo9wQr9ff-eDsSDF6oNDbCEBAI
1795398055529500	codex	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ya	{"description":"Sol assembled the first shift from the README, format reference, integration guide and evidence guide. Add one README-linked runnable walkthrough in a fresh temporary directory using the reference caller. Start with a short owner approval and the generated template. Set a required named target and two required counters, archive and pin the reviewed bytes, preview and spend once. Narrow the approval mid-shift, show the stale pin refused, then run a permitted unit against the reviewed revision.\n\nFinish with a handoff keyed by caller reference. Show which records substantiate a refusal, each ledger debit, and each completed callback, and how to recognize missing execution evidence. Exercise the published commands against the built CLI and assert two debits, the expected totals, and the caller outcomes. Link to the existing guides for the underlying contracts.","kind":"task","labels":["docs"],"size":"s","title":"Walk a first-time operator from written approval through shift handoff"}	blake3:bUJqYo1SHtW9TqBeJU7-E7tbbMKYAiJVW6Wb37-yWGI
1795398062446010	codex	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2ya	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3ac"}	blake3:5yb2LdjbQoPPWF9_-xpIe-lk0IafN3Kgt-VfnL_uz4Y
1795398963976757	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-yum	{"description":"An independent evaluation on 2026-11-22 ran diff on an amendment that added a ceiling. Text output was `ceilings.bytes: None -> Some(5) [review required]`. JSON output was `{\"after\":\"Some(5)\",\"before\":\"None\",\"field\":\"ceilings.bytes\",...}`. A hostname edit showed `Some(\"sip.acme.example\") -> Some(\"sip2.acme.example\")`. A network change was labeled `destination[web-dmz].address`. Ports showed as `443-443,8443-8443`. src/review.rs formats values with {:?} (around lines 118, 174 and 216).\n\nThe owner reviews this output, and it is part of the JSON contract. Show absent values as `none` in text and as null in JSON. Show present values as their plain value: a number stays a number and a string has no Debug quoting. Name the field by its kind (address, network or names). Print ports the way status does (`443, 8443`).\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests cover an added ceiling, a removed ceiling, a hostname change, a network change and a port-list change, in both text and JSON. No output contains `Some(` or `None`.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Show plain values and the real field name in diff output"}	blake3:qGm0gezU6Qq8u_YU9G5cUrw9QFoWakuLjYP6PojgtMU
1795398985583695	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ca	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, a pass had a /24 nameless destination on tcp/1-1024. It also had a host destination inside that /24, restricted to names = [\"app.example.com\"]. `hallpass check` printed only OK. `permits --to 198.51.100.20:443/tcp --name evil.example.net` was allowed by the /24 rule. Only `hallpass lint` reports the name-restriction-overlap finding (bn-2vs). The template and the init help send users to check, not to lint.\n\nShow the overlap finding from check and from status, as a warning or advice that names both labels and points to lint for details. A names list with no effect is the kind of mistake check exists to catch. Do not make the pass invalid: overlapping rules are legal.\n\nAcceptance: the fixture above shows the warning in check and status text and JSON. Disjoint fixtures show none. check still exits 0. Existing lint output stays the same.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Warn in check when another destination defeats a names restriction"}	blake3:uVwZkDwuMziS8chJZVu8SIQQgasmdVhbMxFUSSEZMRk
1795398985728556	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1gc	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `hallpass --format json spend base.toml req` with no --to returned result allowed with target null and warnings []. The text output is just `RECORDED: 1 req (...)`. A pentester who forgets --to, or a wrapper bug that drops it, records debits that were never checked against scope, and nothing says so. require_target_v1 makes a target mandatory, but owners must opt in. The uncommitted template change adds it only as a commented-out line.\n\nWhen a spend succeeds without a target on a pass that does not set require_target_v1, add a warning saying no destination was checked and how to require one. Decide whether `init` should write require_target_v1 = true uncommented. Record the decision in docs/DESIGN.md.\n\nAcceptance: a targetless spend shows the warning in text and in JSON warnings. A targeted spend shows none. With require_target_v1 set, a targetless spend is still refused with target-required. Update the conformance fixtures if the warnings list is part of the contract.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Warn when a spend debits without checking a destination"}	blake3:sLk7fly1mPF-iPG_cWmuDGfYb4WE0znppH9A5XClj24
1795398985858995	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2n0	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, permits, status and spend all re-verified the whole ledger on every call. Measured per call:\n\n- fresh pass: about 0.9 ms\n- 1,886 entries: about 2.9 ms\n- 17,440 entries (4.4 MB): about 21 ms\n\nSpends serialize under the lock. While the ledger grew past 15k entries, 16 workers together reached only about 25–50 spends per second. Extrapolated, a call takes about 120 ms at 100k entries. Ceilings go up to 10^12, so a web tester who counts every request will reach that range. docs/DESIGN.md says the cost is linear but not what it means in practice.\n\nFirst profile where the time goes (hashing, JSON parsing or I/O) and fix the cheap parts. Then decide whether to verify incrementally from a trusted point, for example the witness's seq and hash. Incremental verification must not weaken detection of a changed middle line, a cut tail, or an older backup put back. Today the witness catches all three. If some detection moves to a periodic or explicit full verify, state that in DESIGN.md. Either way, document a practical ledger size and recommend --n batching for high-rate callers.\n\nAcceptance: a benchmark or ignored test builds ledgers of 1k, 10k and 100k entries and reports per-call permits and spend latency. The tamper tests in tests/accounting_history.rs and tests/nasty.rs still pass.","kind":"task","labels":["performance"],"size":"m","title":"Keep per-call cost flat as the ledger grows"}	blake3:OIBVoPpqVafBVeWRVooQNCcPLUucWnsgrWe8AoYQZhM
1795398986084670	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1da	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, the main docs read as if sections had been appended over time:\n\n- docs/INTEGRATING.md opens with review and case suites (SSH signing, mutation testing) and with mapping written approvals. Its own introduction (\"This page is for the author of a tool that sends traffic…\") starts around line 110.\n- docs/spec.md opens with Approval review before Purpose. Large 0.2 sections are appended at the end.\n- The spec's refusal codes list omits target-required, duplicate-reference and reference-inconclusive.\n- The spec's spend JSON field list omits targets, refused_target, charges, receipt and projected_spent.\n- The spec's command and exit-code table omits most of the newer commands.\n\nPut the integration introduction and \"the one call\" first in INTEGRATING.md. Move review and signing to later sections. Open spec.md with Purpose and fold the 0.2 sections into the topic sections they extend. Bring the refusal codes, reply fields and command table up to date. Preferably add a test that compares those lists with the source, so they cannot drift again.\n\nThis is separate from bn-2ya, which adds a new walkthrough. This bone fixes the existing reference docs.","kind":"task","labels":["docs"],"size":"m","title":"Reorder the spec and integration guide and bring their lists up to date"}	blake3:5AOcETsKBWFeLeYelhYH0GVGqRc-zJTwMWJQW-beU5s
1795398986279039	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3nd	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, the fields in a spend reply depended on which flags were passed. A wrapper author had to piece the shape together from three documents.\n\n- A plain scalar spend has no charges or receipt.\n- A scalar spend with --caller-ref has both the scalar fields and charges/receipt.\n- A --charge spend has no counter, n, spent or total.\n- receipt.targets omits name when it is null, but the top-level targets list includes name: null.\n\nDecide on one reply shape. Always include charges, even for a single scalar charge. Always emit name: null in receipt.targets too. Keep the scalar fields for compatibility, or deprecate them explicitly. Document the shape in one place (see the docs bone for spec.md). Update docs/conformance/check.py and its fixtures.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests assert the same set of keys for scalar, scalar with --caller-ref, multi-charge and dry-run spends.","kind":"task","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Return the same spend reply fields regardless of flags"}	blake3:2NuEVRHkhtKTb1t-LU1KJxaAFx_v65FBU9kV6UCepFE
1795399008966219	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-207	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, several advanced commands printed raw JSON fragments as their text output:\n\n- compare-backups: `Captured witness {\"hash\":...,\"seq\":4,...}`, `Ancestry {\"maximal_groups\":[],\"prefix_links\":[]}`, and one JSON object per candidate.\n- reconcile: `{\"caller_ref\":\"dup-0\",\"candidate_count\":1,\"result\":\"mismatch\",...}`.\n- audit-receipts.\n- audit-charges: `Mismatches {\"count\":0,...}`.\n- test-pass --strength: `\"web-dmz\": \"destination[...]...\" -> \"survived\"`.\n- The ledger note column for debits.\n\ncompare-backups --ancestry also printed empty prefix_links and maximal_groups for three successive backups that are prefixes of the witness. Only extended candidates are compared, so an empty list reads as \"no relationships\". Say that explicitly in the text output.\n\nGive each of these commands a table or one line per item in text mode, in the style of status and ledger. Leave the JSON output unchanged.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests show that no text output from these commands contains `{\"`.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"m","title":"Give the advanced commands readable text output"}	blake3:VkjmHvrzQP7wnGIIBxBRAbsF7AlDft1vt6wzEH8kKtY
1795399009092152	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-30s	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, a manifest unit whose bytes charge differed from the ledger produced `{\"caller_ref\":\"dup-0\",\"candidate_count\":1,\"result\":\"mismatch\",\"sequences\":[2],\"truncated\":false}`. Neither the text nor the JSON said whether the digest, a charge, a target or the reference differed. The operator had to diff the ledger line against the manifest by hand.\n\nAdd a differences list to each mismatch row, with the field, the expected value and the recorded value. Cover digest, each charge counter and amount, targets, and name. With several candidates, show differences for each candidate or for the closest one, and say which.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests change one field at a time (digest, one charge amount, an extra charge, a target, a name) and assert exactly one difference naming that field, in text and JSON.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Say what differed in a reconcile mismatch"}	blake3:AE-MRl8ELr00apzJtOPXu7qEYyrhmO6mlfIi3YoLFA0
1795399009231737	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-rhc	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, error messages escaped backslashes twice:\n\n- `hallpass permits base.toml --to 192.0.2.10:443/tcp --name 'bücher.example.com'` prints: `b\\\\u{fc}cher.example.com` has non-ASCII characters\n- A pass with `address = \"1...\"` prints: is written with the escape `\\\\u` ... Only \\\\\" and \\\\\\\\ are accepted as escapes\n\nThe expected text is `b\\u{fc}cher…` and `Only \\\" and \\\\ are accepted`. Escape the offending text once, when it is put into the message. JSON serialization will escape it again, which is correct. Check the other places that quote user input in errors.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests for both messages in text mode, plus a JSON test in which error.message, once parsed, contains a single backslash.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Escape backslashes once in error messages"}	blake3:ElR-SSRo19ug2DCXca3GIdXQO2UE-P9-ualsfWwRee4
1795399009395717	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3fd	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `hallpass permits base.toml --to localhost:8080/tcp` said: \"a name may go in the informational `hostname` field\". That is advice for writing a pass file, not for a query. In a pass file, `address = \"app.example.com\"` gets the same hint. For something like a CDN front, the field that enforces anything is `names`, so the hint points users to a field that does nothing.\n\nFor --to: say to resolve the name, ask with the literal IP, and pass the name with --name. For pass files: mention names (which restricts) along with hostname (which is informational only).\n\nAcceptance: update the CLI tests for both messages.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Give query-appropriate advice when --to names a host"}	blake3:HnUI9g897Ee46FhC9i9xyme9LXQmgLUSLj240VFsKzU
1795399009534089	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1j6	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, a regression suite had a case for an address just outside a /28. `test-pass --strength` generated only the \"remove names\" and \"add port ±1\" mutations. It never widened the network, changed the protocol or moved the window. Out-of-scope cases therefore earned no credit, and the report looked weaker than the suite was.\n\nAdd these loosening mutations:\n\n- widen each network prefix by one bit\n- turn a single address into its /31 (or /127 for IPv6)\n- allow the other transport\n- move valid_from earlier and valid_until later\n- raise each ceiling\n\nKeep the existing reporting format. Make sure each mutation only loosens the pass and the mutated pass still validates. A mutation that would fail check should be skipped and reported as skipped, not as survived.\n\nAcceptance: docs/review-suite-strong.toml kills the new mutations. A suite with no out-of-scope cases shows them as survived. tests/mutation_strength.rs covers each mutation kind.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"m","title":"Mutate networks, protocols and windows in test-pass --strength"}	blake3:HZTml-sNwX7VZfLsVfdi886dMT3w3u_EQPlqAlSKcBQ
1795399009674829	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1if	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, a pass had two destinations labeled \"a\". The second also had `network = \"10.0.0.0/7\"`. check reported only the /7 breadth problem. The duplicate label appeared only after the network was fixed. check promises to list every problem at once.\n\nCheck label uniqueness over the raw destination list, separately from whether each destination is otherwise valid.\n\nAcceptance: a check test on that fixture lists both problems.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Report duplicate labels even when a destination has other errors"}	blake3:9q6U6KGSU3xivdXqi2JS_oQrtW4cY9L9SqBhqr5H-Oo
1795399009821001	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3he	{"description":"An independent evaluation on 2026-11-22 found several small inconsistencies. Each is cheap to fix:\n\n- witness-unavailable errors carry `recording: null`, while lock-timeout says `\"not-recorded\"`. Use the same value whenever the outcome is known.\n- `diff --containment --at now` fails with \"the 'year' component could not be parsed\". Accept `now` as other commands do, or say that it is not accepted.\n- init errors end with \"Nothing was permitted.\", which makes no sense for init. Drop the suffix for commands that never permit anything.\n- receipt text shows nanosecond timestamps, while ledger shows whole seconds. Use one style in text output.\n\nAcceptance: a CLI test for each item.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Smooth small CLI inconsistencies found in evaluation"}	blake3:dUY_UYwPEgp2ZDkfliRIVcZixzPpeb_GPyYZQycObiU
1795399009852082	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-3hs	{"body":"An independent evaluation on 2026-11-22 reproduced this: after one spend, status reports a matching witness, and checkpoint --format json still warns \"The witness corroborates a prefix and may lag the exported tail.\" The evaluation also found a related case. checkpoint on a valid pass with no ledger yet returns {\"error\":{\"code\":\"io\",\"message\":\"c.toml.ledger: no such file; check the path\"}}. It should report an empty history (seq 0), or a clear \"nothing recorded yet\" result, instead of an I/O error. Cover that case together with the matching, lagging, absent and contradictory cases."}	blake3:LGBrZXjfJxpmWDVLWMxpdh4RVfO6zY7wy20IOFUEppQ
