1795406272643785	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-2n0	{"state":"done"}	blake3:3D3-P9brCC1K5Zl3coEjEHqonqZM7yN34RFEqLq2jOM
1795408670705814	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1mk	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, a pass's ledger was used with a fresh state directory, as when an engagement moves to another machine. Every `spend --caller-ref X --require-new-ref` was refused with reference-inconclusive, because \"the nonempty ledger has no local witness\". The refusal records no witness, so the next guarded spend is refused the same way. After `hallpass adopt` (which reported \"Carried from the witness: nothing\" and kept every earlier line), every later guarded spend was refused with \"the ledger contains an adoption, which may have lost earlier references\". That lasts for the rest of the engagement, and no command acknowledges it. The reference caller always passes --require-new-ref, so it can never spend on that pass again. The guard in src/ledger/reference.rs treats any adoption_count > 0 as inconclusive.\n\nMake the guard inconclusive only when references can really be missing: an adoption that started a new ledger or carried totals from the witness that the ledger's own lines do not account for. An adoption that appended to a complete ledger loses nothing. Separately, the refusal message must name the way out: for a ledger with no witness, adopting establishes one. Keep fail-closed behaviour for an adoption that did lose lines. Document the rule in the spec section on caller references.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests cover a moved ledger plus an adoption that carried nothing, where the next new reference is accepted and a repeated reference is still refused as duplicate. They also cover an adoption that carried totals with no backing lines, where new references stay inconclusive and the message names the next step. Re-run tests/reference_caller.rs.","kind":"bug","labels":["accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Let guarded spends continue after an adoption that lost no references"}	blake3:mxR70bO82TTYr2xHQioNW6uGf0zVpJsDHWRqtt-zX1M
1795408670836142	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-gpe	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, the documented review flow was followed. The tester copied the reviewed pass to reviewed.toml, edited the live pass (lowering a ceiling), and ran `hallpass diff --budget-impact reviewed.toml p.toml`. diff read the ledger beside OLD (reviewed.toml.ledger), found it missing, and printed the witness error that ends \"run `hallpass adopt reviewed.toml --reason ...`\". Following that advice created a second ledger, re-pointed the witness, and broke the live pass: every status or spend on p.toml then failed with \"ledger ... was changed outside hallpass\", until another adopt.\n\nCompute budget impact from the live ledger. Use the ledger beside NEW, which in the documented retrieve-then-diff flow is the live pass, or the only one of the two that exists, and say which ledger was used in text and JSON. If neither has a ledger and the witness records spends, report that read-only without adoption advice. A read-only command must never suggest a write that can fork the ledger. Check the other read-only commands (diff, lint, receipt, reconcile, audit-*, compare-backups) for the same adopt advice.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests cover --budget-impact with only NEW's ledger present, with only OLD's present, and with neither present. None of the outputs contains \"hallpass adopt\".","kind":"bug","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Read budget impact from the live ledger and never suggest adopt from diff"}	blake3:GM_SAerIATkd6QtOSFdxFEE_CufJJ0z7Qm1--EUI8AA
1795408671012436	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2gn	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, docs/reference-caller/caller.py ran `run ... --to '[2001:DB8::10]:443/tcp'`. hallpass accepted and debited the target and replied with its canonical form, [2001:db8::10]. inspect_reply compared that with the raw string, so the caller journaled \"spend outcome unknown, nothing sent\" while the budget was spent. `handoff` then failed with \"reconcile failed: manifest target or name is not canonical\" (exit 1), so the shift could not be handed off. The caller lowercases names (target() around line 98) but never canonicalizes the address literal. A Bun wrapper written by the same tester hit the same trap with `@API.example.com`.\n\nIn caller.py, canonicalize each --to before journaling the intent: lower-case IPv6, compress zeros, lower-case and strip the trailing dot of a name. Alternatively, refuse non-canonical input before spending. Either way the intent, the spend and the manifest must use one spelling. Also consider a CLI aid so that every wrapper doesn't reimplement this. For example, `permits` already returns the normalized `target` and `name` in JSON, so INTEGRATING.md could tell callers to take the canonical form from a preflight permits reply.\n\nAcceptance: a tests/reference_caller.rs case with an upper-case, uncompressed IPv6 target and an upper-case name produces a verified debit, a sent unit and a clean handoff.","kind":"bug","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Canonicalize targets in the reference caller before spending"}	blake3:Wx3zyGL-RdA1vJvHc3n3KspQRPkD6O6v5pe7cKUzFFg
1795408671158427	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3o7	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `hallpass audit-charges p.toml /nonexist` printed \"Charge audit incomplete ... Gap archive-missing 1\" and exited 2, as if the archive existed but lacked the revision. audit-receipts behaves the same way. `retrieve /nonexist ...` correctly says \"no such file; check the path\" and exits 1.\n\nBefore auditing, check that the archive directory exists, is a directory and is private. Otherwise return an io (or untrusted-owner) error with exit 1. A typo in the path should not look like missing evidence.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests for both audit commands with a missing path, a file instead of a directory, and a world-readable directory.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Report a missing archive directory as an error in the audit commands"}	blake3:c58KGbZxT6cl51AmJEUHc_7JJ7ypeUTeG3zFyetAA1w
1795408671290847	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-iyn	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, a ledger of 29,533 debits, each with --caller-ref, --record-target, a name and a note (13.4 MB, about 450 B per line, on tmpfs), cost about 35 ms per call for status, permits, spend, receipt, capacity and checkpoint. Eight concurrent spenders reached about 30 spends per second, with a p50 of 255 ms. docs/DESIGN.md (\"Cost per call\") predicts about 14 ms at that size from its 0.45 µs per line figure, which was measured on about 297 B lines with fewer receipts.\n\nProfile this line mix. Likely suspects are lines that miss the direct reader in src/ledger/written.rs (names, notes, real-clock timestamps, field spellings), receipt validation, and evidence collection for receipt and reference checks. Fix what is cheap without weakening per-call verification. Then restate the DESIGN.md cost per byte and per line for realistic receipt-bearing lines, and extend tests/ledger_scale.rs with that line mix.\n\nAcceptance: tests/ledger_scale.rs reports figures for a receipt-and-note mix. DESIGN.md states figures that match it.","kind":"task","labels":["performance"],"size":"m","title":"Measure per-call cost on ledgers with receipts and notes"}	blake3:EaB4DIYMCEQx2H22I-SroN_fvFJXsSNyCDgr-n1Jy0g
1795408671420095	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20u	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `hallpass ledger --format json` on a 29.5k-line engagement printed 10.6 MB. An operator checking the last few debits during a shift has no way to limit the output.\n\nAdd `--tail N` and `--since SEQ` to `ledger`, for text and JSON. The whole chain must still be verified before anything is printed; only the output is limited. Say in the output that it is a slice (for example `\"shown\": {\"from\": .., \"to\": ..}`) so a reader does not mistake it for the full history. Update the help, spec.md and the doc-list test.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests for --tail and --since, including a tampered earlier line, which still fails.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Let ledger show only recent entries"}	blake3:XwK8yTeq4l1KBPa4Kw096AmcEslPJvnhl2U35ZBb9k0
1795408671535662	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-27f	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `permits --duration 60` with 60 s of the window left was refused: \"the work may take up to 60 s, but only 60 s of the approval window remain\". The same call with --duration 59 is allowed. valid_until is exclusive, so work that would end exactly at valid_until is refused, but the message reads as if 60 should fit.\n\nWord the refusal to match the rule, for example: \"the work may take up to 60 s and would end at or after the window closes (valid until ...)\". The same refusal appears in spend, preview, capacity and rehearse; keep them consistent.\n\nAcceptance: update the CLI test for the boundary case to assert the new wording.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Make the window-too-short message agree with its threshold"}	blake3:y9jQjZj3gzN5G_1_jN_1AB_5YenayRG9TP5LzA51Uh8
1795408671658149	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2f2	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `FORMAT=yaml hallpass status p.toml` failed with \"invalid value 'yaml' for '--format <FORMAT>'\", and `FORMAT=json` silently switched an operator's text output to JSON. src/main.rs reads the unprefixed FORMAT environment variable (around line 1931). FORMAT is a generic name that other tools and shell setups use.\n\nRead HALLPASS_FORMAT instead. Decide whether to keep FORMAT for one release with a deprecation warning, and record that in CHANGELOG.md. An invalid value should say which variable it came from.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests show that HALLPASS_FORMAT selects the format, FORMAT is ignored (or only warns), and an invalid HALLPASS_FORMAT names the variable. Update the help text and spec.md.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Read the default output format from HALLPASS_FORMAT, not FORMAT"}	blake3:xJTMRqsjdbJSTT0KF5ZAln68OTL4pfXfU-L96FQjJ18
1795408671770954	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3h7	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `hallpass --format json spend p.toml calls --n x --to 192.0.2.10:443/tcp` returned \"command\": null although the subcommand was known. The error object had only code, line and message (no ledger, recording or seq), and the parser's error text was also printed to stderr. A hallpass-level usage error such as `--to bad` returns \"command\": \"spend\" and the full error key set.\n\nFor clap parse errors, fill in `command` when the subcommand was recognized. Always emit the full error key set, with recording \"not-recorded\", since nothing ran. In JSON mode, print nothing to stderr. Extend tests/doc_lists.rs, which checks that documented keys match real replies, to cover a parse error.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests for a bad value, an unknown flag and an unknown subcommand. The JSON has the same keys as a hallpass usage error, and stderr is empty in JSON mode.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Give argument parse errors the same JSON shape as other errors"}	blake3:VspLDkZVlGsf8AlETVeDPKnJw5bgmRx6KSGJ6y6MzDM
1795408671887192	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-202	{"description":"In an independent evaluation on 2026-11-22, `diff --containment` reported a single counterexample for a widening that exposed several addresses (a /29+/29 merged into a /28). `diff --examples` listed many refused→refused changes where only the refusal code changed, which is noise for a reviewer looking for new permissions.\n\nLet --containment report up to a bounded number of counterexamples, one per distinct widened destination or port range, and say when more exist. In --examples, put changes that newly allow something first, and summarize refused→refused code changes as a count, or show them only with a flag.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests show a widening across two separate ranges giving two counterexamples, and an --examples run whose allowed-changes section comes before any refusal-code changes.","kind":"task","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Show several containment counterexamples and quiet unchanged refusals in diff --examples"}	blake3:9tlUfx4skO16b0cQHfChlqBn3EoijKUpeOPsvfsTH9Y
1795408672004265	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-nxt	{"description":"An independent evaluation on 2026-11-22 found small wording problems:\n\n- `--to 0xcb007101:443/tcp` is called \"a host name\" and gets \"resolve the name yourself\" advice. docs/PASS-FORMAT.md treats that spelling as a numeric IPv4 form. Say it is a non-standard IPv4 spelling and ask for dotted decimal.\n- A ledger-file mode error ends \"check that its content is what the owner approved\". The ledger is written by hallpass, not by the owner. Use ledger-appropriate advice.\n- Some messages run into the suffix without a full stop, for example \"...(for example 10.0.0.1) Nothing was permitted.\" Make sure every message ends in a full stop before the suffix.\n\nAcceptance: a CLI test for each item.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"xs","title":"Fix small message wording found in the second evaluation"}	blake3:jgWN3oENNLIlDls3ZXxm0x5kTGWwK7GCxwd0u_c8h6Q
