1795411795996748	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-iyn	{"state":"done"}	blake3:v1Q3R1GyfHwG2dimw3nSWUIXXQ5XTSJeRhClBNvlvbg
1795451922877182	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-11m	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nA common mid-engagement event: the owner sends an amended approval as a new file. The operator saves it as `proposed.toml` next to `acme.toml`, with the same `id`. The first spend under the new file fails with `ledger-missing`. The message is byte for byte the same as for a ledger that is really lost. It says \"if it is really lost, run `hallpass adopt proposed.toml`\", or \"give it its own id\" (which means a zero budget). `adopt --preview` shows a normal ledger-missing preview and does not mention that the witnessed ledger is intact.\n\nFollowing the adopt advice does lasting damage:\n- It starts a second ledger, so the original becomes `ledger-changed`.\n- Under the bn-1mk rule, the adoption carries totals, so every later `--require-new-ref` spend for that id is `reference-inconclusive`. The reference caller always guards, so all of its units stop.\n\nRepro (debug build, HALLPASS_UNSAFE_TEST_CLOCK inside the window): spend 3 times on p.toml; `sed 's/calls = 100/calls = 200/' p.toml > q.toml`; `hallpass spend q.toml calls --to ...`. The error names `p.toml.ledger` as \"last seen\", but advises moving it or adopting.\n\nWanted:\n- When the witness's last-seen ledger path still exists, verifies, and matches the witness (same seq and tail), report a distinct situation rather than a lost ledger. The new pass file belongs to a ledger that is alive at PATH. If it is a revision of that approval, replace the old pass file's content with the new one in place (or move the ledger only if the old file is being retired). Do not adopt.\n- This could be a new error code (for example `ledger-elsewhere`), or keep `ledger-missing` with a field that says the last-seen ledger is intact. Choose one and document it in the spec's error code list, with the reason.\n- `adopt --preview` and `adopt` must say the same thing. Adopt should refuse (or require an explicit flag) when the last-seen ledger is intact and verifies, because the adoption would fork the history.\n- The lost case, where the last-seen ledger is really gone or does not verify, keeps today's advice.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests for the second-file case (spend, status, adopt --preview, adopt) and for the really-lost case, which keeps its current advice. Update docs/spec.md (witness table and adopt section), INTEGRATING.md recovery text and, if needed, WALKTHROUGH.md.","kind":"bug","labels":["recovery","accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Recognize a second pass file that shares an id with a live ledger"}	blake3:8pm9oI1mZpK1FxTiKCtFus0UMYAun-8NSvFlDIVSQRI
1795451923020779	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1pt	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nAdding a destination to a pass is the most dangerous kind of change, yet plain `hallpass diff OLD NEW` prints only `introduced destination: cdn-any`. It shows no address or network, protocol or ports, and no `[review required]` marker. The walkthrough teaches reviewers to scan for that marker. JSON has `review_required: true`, but `changes` is empty. `check` warns about the name-restriction overlap, but diff does not.\n\nRepro: an old pass with a named destination `cdn-web` on 203.0.113.20 (names = [...]); a new pass that adds `[[destination]] label = \"cdn-any\" network = \"203.0.113.20/32\" protocol = \"tcp\" ports = 443`. `hallpass diff old.toml new.toml`.\n\nWanted:\n- Text: an introduced destination prints its full grant (address or network, protocol, ports, names or \"any name\") and `[review required]`. A removed destination prints what it granted.\n- JSON: `changes` (or a sibling list) describes the introduced or removed destination with the same values, so a JSON reviewer sees what was added.\n- When the new destination defeats another destination's `names` (the existing name-restriction overlap check), diff says so in both formats.\n- Keep existing keys. A new key or a new `changes` row kind is fine; document it.\n\nAcceptance: tests in a new test file for introduced and removed destinations in text and JSON, including the overlap warning. Update docs/spec.md and the WALKTHROUGH if its sample output changes.","kind":"bug","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Show what an introduced destination grants in plain diff output"}	blake3:j7VQG1-GMzcSjC3x81Xb_XJYiOSMHqH7AcJuMCVjfxg
1795451923148420	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3i1	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\n`python3 docs/reference-caller/caller.py handoff JOURNAL PASS` lists every ledger debit whose caller reference is not in this journal as \"no record\". Each of those gets \"ATTENTION: journal and ledger disagree\" and makes the exit code 2. Several workers sharing a pass's ledger is the normal case. In the evaluation, a shift's handoff printed 335 ATTENTION rows, 333 of which were other workers' legitimate debits.\n\nCode: docs/reference-caller/caller.py `handoff()`, the loop over `ledger[\"entries\"]` that appends `(ref or \"(no reference)\", \"no record\", \"match\", ...)`.\n\nWanted:\n- By default, the handoff judges only this journal's units. Other debits are summarised in one line (for example \"N other debits by other callers or without a reference, not judged\"), and they never set the exit code.\n- An option to list them, for example `--all`. A way to bound the report, for example `--since SEQ`, which can use the new `ledger --since`. An optional reference prefix that this caller owns (for example `--ref-prefix shift-17-`) can turn foreign debits that carry this caller's prefix but have no journal record back into attention items, because those are the dangerous ones.\n- Update docs/INTEGRATING.md (handoff text) and the reference caller README/comments.\n\nAcceptance: a test in tests/reference_caller.rs (or a new test file) with two journals writing to one ledger. Each handoff exits 0 and names only its own units. A debit carrying this caller's prefix with no journal record is still flagged.","kind":"bug","labels":["integration"],"size":"s","title":"Let the reference caller's handoff report only this caller's units on a shared ledger"}	blake3:35YZGm2xHZHU449dMRknCyr_LcRzczCcYO3hJESExhQ
1795451923272500	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-dqi	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nEvery call verifies the whole ledger, so cost grows with ledger size. The evaluator measured about 1 ms per spend at 1k lines, 6 ms at 10k and 12 ms at 20k. With 8 writers, aggregate throughput fell from about 2,400 spends/s on an empty ledger to about 44/s at 42k lines. docs/DESIGN.md (\"Cost per call\") documents this, with the guidance to batch with `--n` and keep a ledger under about 100k lines / 50 MB.\n\nBut INTEGRATING.md, README.md, WALKTHROUGH.md and the reference caller say to spend \"before each unit of work (one call, one request)\" and never mention cost, batching or the size limit. A high-rate integrator learns this only in production.\n\nWanted:\n- A short section in INTEGRATING.md: the cost model (about 1.2 ms per MB plus process start), when one spend per unit is fine, and when to batch (`--n`, or `--charge` with a larger amount per unit of work), with the safety trade-off of batching (reserved but unused budget).\n- When to split an engagement across passes. Link to DESIGN.md for the numbers.\n- One sentence in README and in the reference caller's notes pointing there.\n\nAcceptance: the docs only; doc tests (walkthrough, doc_lists) still pass.","kind":"task","labels":["docs","performance"],"size":"xs","title":"Tell integrators what each call costs and when to batch"}	blake3:6pj9yqNH10c-6Cf8ewsiDNk4cxhRdvkK4nzq4bG2yMM
1795451923389092	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2hj	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nWith `required_counters_v1 = [\"requests\", \"bytes\"]` and ceilings requests, bytes, scans:\n\n`hallpass spend rc.toml scan --to 192.0.2.10:443/tcp` (a typo for `scans`) is refused with \"every debit must charge `bytes`; include its positive cost\". The real mistake, that `scan` is not a ceiling, is reported only after the operator adds the required counters. The refusal also lists \"1 scan (0 spent, unknown left)\".\n\nWanted:\n- Check counter names (`unknown-counter`) before required counters (`required-counter-missing`), so the typo is named first, in spend, the dry run, permits (if it checks counters), rehearse and capacity alike.\n- Update the refusal order in docs/spec.md if it is documented, and keep the order the same in the library and the CLI.\n- PASS-FORMAT.md: state plainly that `required_counters_v1` applies to every debit under the pass. Show how to express \"every request must also charge bytes\" when the pass has other kinds of work, such as scans: charge the required counters on those units too, or use a separate pass. The evaluator could not express the owner's \"for every request\" rule and found this out by trial.\n\nAcceptance: a CLI test for the typo'd counter on a pass with required counters, asserting `unknown-counter`.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli","accounting"],"size":"xs","title":"Report an unknown counter before a missing required counter"}	blake3:h7WE69RoyrPzf9eUrwE-gDkO0n0nopyMMvZHZehSX-E
1795451923504712	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-385	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nWhen another process holds the writer lock (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/hallpass/<id>.lock`), `spend` without `--lock-timeout-ms` waits forever and prints nothing. Repro: `flock $XDG_STATE_HOME/hallpass/ID.lock sleep 30 &` then `timeout 2 hallpass spend PASS ...`. It is killed with no output. With `--lock-timeout-ms 300`, the error is \"timed out acquiring the writer lock on PATH; this spend recorded no debit.\" It gives no next step.\n\nWanted:\n- After a short wait (for example 2 s), print one line to stderr in text mode: waiting for the writer lock on PATH, held by another hallpass process (by PID if it can be found cheaply and portably; otherwise say how to find it, for example `fuser PATH`). In JSON mode stdout stays one object; put the notice on stderr or in `warnings`, and say which in the spec.\n- The `lock-timeout` message ends with what to do: retry, or find and stop the process holding PATH. It also says that a crashed holder releases the lock automatically, if that is true of the flock-based lock (check it).\n- Consider a documented default timeout for interactive use only if it is safe. Waiting is correct for scripts, so do not change the default without stating the reason.\n\nAcceptance: a test that holds the lock and checks the waiting notice, and a test for the timeout message.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Say when spend is waiting for the writer lock, and what to do on a lock timeout"}	blake3:tCqAUMysb7JnH6fs2lA9rHRPWEPi9Hmf9kHOBBC9Qzs
1795451923626992	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-31y	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nChanging `\"n\":1` to `\"n\":9` on file line 3 of a 6-line ledger gives: \"the ledger d.toml.ledger is damaged at line 4: the line does not follow the one before it (a line was changed, removed or moved). ... compare it with your notes, and remove only a final line that was cut short (that spend was never confirmed).\"\n\n- The edited line is 3. The chain break shows at line 4, because line 4's `prev` no longer matches. The message names only line 4.\n- The advice about a final line cut short does not apply to a break in the middle of the file.\n\nWanted:\n- For a chain break (`prev` mismatch), say that line N does not follow line N-1, so one of them was changed (or a line between them was removed or moved).\n- When the break is not at the last line, the advice is: restore the ledger from a backup, `hallpass compare-backups` to find the good copy, and do not edit lines by hand. Keep the cut-short advice for a damaged final line only.\n- Keep the error code. If it adds a JSON field (for example the two line numbers), document it.\n\nAcceptance: CLI tests for a mid-file edit and for a truncated last line, each asserting its own advice.","kind":"bug","labels":["recovery"],"size":"xs","title":"Name the right lines and give backup advice when a ledger is damaged mid-file"}	blake3:b3aU4xf0cF4GUC4wTZ4DnH98Mzqt_XYiOUUzRnjJ60o
1795451923745148	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-qu0	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nOne upper-case name in a `reconcile` manifest gives \"manifest target or name is not canonical\", with no row index, caller reference, field or value. With hundreds of units, the operator has to bisect the manifest.\n\nCode: src/ledger/evidence_manifest.rs, around \"manifest target or name is not canonical\". Other manifest validation errors are likely the same.\n\nWanted:\n- Each manifest validation error names the unit (index and caller_ref), the field (for example `targets[2].name`), the offending value (escaped for display) and the canonical form when one exists (for example \"write `app.example.com`\"). The message ends with what to do.\n\nAcceptance: tests for a non-canonical name, a non-canonical address and one other manifest error, each asserting the row and field.","kind":"bug","labels":["audit"],"size":"xs","title":"Name the row, field and value in a reconcile manifest error"}	blake3:TnJnhuovj1fwxe4CvsrluBycQXwSFNJFGA0nyIIExWk
1795451923865034	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-gwp	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nTwo inconsistencies between commands that report the same facts:\n\n1. Times. A real-clock debit is written with nanoseconds (`\"time\":\"2026-11-23T16:36:15.36549262Z\"`). `ledger --json` reports `2026-11-23T16:36:23Z`, but `receipt --json` and `audit-receipts` report the full recorded value. spec.md says \"Times are UTC, whole seconds\" (in the `window` paragraph), which reads as if it applies everywhere.\n   Pick one rule and apply it to every command that reports a recorded ledger time: ledger, receipt, reconcile, audit-receipts, audit-charges, checkpoint and diff --budget-impact if it shows any. Recommended: JSON gives the time exactly as recorded, because the ledger is evidence and truncation loses ordering; text may print whole seconds. Document the rule in the spec and give the reason in the changelog report, since JSON values change.\n\n2. Copy relation. An older, shorter copy of a ledger is `mismatch` to `verify-checkpoint` and `shorter` to `compare-backups`. Give verify-checkpoint a way to say \"this copy is an earlier prefix of the checkpointed ledger\" that uses the same word as compare-backups. Prefer a new field to a new `result` value, so that callers matching on `result` do not break, and document it.\n\nAcceptance: tests asserting the same time string from ledger and receipt for one debit, and the shared relation word for an older copy in both commands.","kind":"bug","labels":["audit","cli"],"size":"s","title":"Make evidence commands agree on debit times and on what an older copy is called"}	blake3:bSVVfLIE5LFWcZDG7WK6tXTCLcPOYr5LfNfBFxrZ3zY
1795451923972512	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-21z	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nVerified against main:\n\n1. docs/spec.md (around line 630) still says parser failures \"print their diagnostic to stderr and return `error.code = \\\"usage\\\"` with null `command` and `file`\". Since bn-3h7, JSON mode prints nothing to stderr and fills `command` when the subcommand was recognized, with `recording: \"not-recorded\"`. Rewrite the paragraph, and add a check to tests/cli_parse.rs or tests/doc_lists.rs that would have caught this.\n2. docs/PASS-FORMAT.md describes `hostname` as \"what the owner calls this destination\" and shows it as free text in the example comment. A value like `\"ExampleCo CDN edge\"` is refused, because it must be a DNS name. Either say it must be a DNS host name (and give the rule), or allow free text. Saying it plainly is the smaller change.\n3. INTEGRATING.md says every `refusal.message` and `error.message` \"ends with what to do next\". The `lock-timeout` and reconcile manifest messages are covered by other bones. Others don't follow the rule either, for example \"`198.51.100.300` is not a literal IPv4 or IPv6 address.\" Audit the AddressError and error.rs messages for a next step and add the missing ones, or soften the claim. Prefer adding the steps.\n4. \"playpen\" appears in spec.md and INTEGRATING.md without an introduction. Add one sentence the first time each document mentions it (what it is, and that it is a separate tool).\n\nAcceptance: doc tests pass, and any message changes come with test updates.","kind":"task","labels":["docs"],"size":"s","title":"Fix the stale and inaccurate statements found in the docs"}	blake3:kujZibkB7KfpCfF62Nia0wVXn7eExcaNJP0Pu0HKxrk
1795451924083297	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-15w	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\nSmall output problems seen in the evaluation:\n\n1. When the owner lowers a ceiling below what is already spent, `status` prints \"calls  3 total, 5 spent, 0 left\" with no flag. It should say clearly that the counter is over its ceiling (for example \"OVER by 2\"). JSON should carry an explicit boolean or overage value. Make `ledger` totals (\"5 spent of 3\") match.\n2. Debit rows (multi-counter `--charge` units) break the column alignment of the `ledger` text table. Keep the columns aligned, or give debits a layout that reads cleanly. The walkthrough runs `ledger` word for word, so update it if its output changes.\n3. The window message showed \"0 s remain\" when about 1 s was left. Check how seconds remaining is rounded in the window-too-short and status messages, and make the text agree with the decision. It must never say 0 s while the window is open, or say 1 s while the work would be refused.\n\nAcceptance: tests for each item.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Flag over-ceiling counters and tidy status and ledger text"}	blake3:PMhIT7mO9yvI8T7jwcsv7TODcLogjUP7inH0zuZlFoo
1795451924203363	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20t	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main.\n\n1. A relative `XDG_STATE_HOME` (for example `XDG_STATE_HOME=relstate`) is ignored, which the XDG spec requires. hallpass then silently uses `$HOME/.local/state/hallpass` and starts a fresh witness there. Print a warning that names the ignored value and the directory used instead. This matters because a fresh witness drops the protection against a lost or rewound ledger.\n2. On a nonempty ledger with no local witness, a guarded spend (`--require-new-ref`) that is refused `reference-inconclusive` also prints the no-witness warning \"the next spend records one\". A refused guarded spend records nothing, and the refusal says to run `hallpass adopt`. Word the warning so that it holds for every command that prints it.\n3. `verify-approval` gives one generic message for a wrong principal, tampered bytes, an untrusted key and a wrong namespace. Where ssh-keygen's result allows, distinguish them, with a next step for each (for example \"the signature is for namespace X; sign with -n hallpass-approval\"). Keep the result code fail-closed and the same.\n\nAcceptance: tests for each item. The verify-approval test can be skipped when ssh-keygen is not installed, as existing tests do.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli","verification"],"size":"s","title":"Warn about an ignored relative state directory and fix misleading warnings"}	blake3:p9ouXjnuMgECsxfJtgrVNq5QnYdEwZEc0EBkz2Ejkb0
1795451932741281	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-11m	{"body":"Related follow-up from bn-1mk: an adoption that carries totals or records a previous witness makes every later --require-new-ref spend reference-inconclusive for the rest of the ledger. adopt --preview and adopt should say so before the operator commits, for example 'after this adoption, guarded spends (--require-new-ref) cannot prove a reference is new; reconcile against your run record'. Fold this into the same change."}	blake3:PpoksqUkl4_lzsI3DTzw1dmF7C46Qb9L294iH-hCgP0
