1795455172479117	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-11m	{"state":"done"}	blake3:6cLeA4f2iralSqzGszfKc8zFkN57OoWIdxrGRfqLKes
1795460008578173	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-23h	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\n**Fail-open hole.** When a pass is saved in place (truncate, then write in chunks, as most editors and `cat > file` do), a concurrent `permits` or `spend` can read a prefix of the file. A prefix that ends at a line boundary is often a valid pass that grants MORE than the approval, because it lacks trailing restrictions.\n\nEvaluator repro (work/conc/torn.py):\n- A pass larger than 4 KiB whose last destination ends with `names = [\"shop.example.com\"]`.\n- Six workers spend `--to 203.0.113.10:443/tcp` with no name and no `--digest`.\n- Meanwhile the file is rewritten in place in 4096-byte writes; only a comment changes between saves.\n- Result: 219 debits recorded as allowed, against 775 `name-required` and 4,587 `invalid-pass`. With `--digest` pinned, 0 were allowed.\n\nLead repro, deterministic: `head -n -1 full.toml > prefix.toml` (the same pass without its `names` line). `spend prefix.toml requests --to 203.0.113.10:443/tcp` is RECORDED. A cut inside a line can also narrow or move a port (`ports = 443` read as `ports = 44`) and still validate.\n\nToday nothing in the read path (files.rs, pass load) checks that the file was stable while it was read.\n\nWanted:\n1. **Make a torn read fail closed.** Options, to evaluate and choose with reasons:\n   - (a) Require the pass file to end with a newline. This catches cuts inside a line but not at a line boundary.\n   - (b) A stability check: after reading, `fstat` the open file. If its mtime is very recent (for example under 1 s), wait briefly, re-read, and require identical bytes and an unchanged size and mtime. Otherwise refuse as `invalid-pass` with \"the pass file is being written; try again\".\n   - (c) Anything stronger you find.\n   At minimum, do (a) and (b). The cost is a short wait only right after an edit. Measure it.\n2. **Docs.** The README, PASS-FORMAT.md, INTEGRATING.md and WALKTHROUGH.md all say to edit the pass in place. Say how to replace it atomically (write `pass.toml.new` in the same directory, `chmod 600`, then `mv` over the old file). The walkthrough's `cat > engagement.pass.toml` amendment becomes write-then-`mv`; it is checked word for word by tests/walkthrough.rs.\n3. **Recommend digest pinning** (`--digest` / `expect_digest`) for callers that spend while the owner may edit, and list the torn-read risk in DESIGN.md's residual risks.\n\nAcceptance: a test that a valid prefix written just now (mtime fresh) is refused, or re-read after it settles; a test that a file without a final newline is refused; tests/walkthrough.rs passes with the atomic replace. Re-run the evaluator's torn.py scenario (copy it from /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval4/work/conc/torn.py) with a debug build and the real clock, and report how many unnamed spends are allowed (target: 0).","kind":"bug","labels":["verification","docs"],"size":"m","title":"Fail closed when a pass file is read while it is being rewritten in place","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:xIukUihz3-5yHLMDHdt3TokxsfVengJQqyzyUECsjPA
1795460008683909	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ck	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\n`diff --containment --at T OLD NEW` returns `result: \"contained\"` and exit 0 for a revision that keeps the endpoints but loosens everything else.\n\nLead repro: from v1.toml, move `valid_until` a month later and raise `requests` from 100 to 100000. Output: the two `[review required]` lines, then \"Endpoint scope at ...: contained\", exit 0, JSON `result: \"contained\"`, `review_required: true`. The evaluator also deleted `require_target_v1` and `required_counters_v1`, with the same result.\n\nA CI gate keyed on the exit code or `result` accepts this revision. WALKTHROUGH.md step 6 says \"The endpoint scope is contained, so the revision only narrows\", which is the wrong inference here.\n\nWanted:\n- The containment verdict must account for every loosening that diff already marks `[review required]`: a later `valid_until` or earlier `valid_from`, a higher or removed ceiling, a removed `require_target_v1` or `required_counters_v1`, an un-revoke, and any other field diff treats as widening.\n- Report it in both formats. For example, keep `containment.result` for endpoints, add an overall verdict (`contained` only when nothing loosens; otherwise `widened`, listing the non-endpoint loosenings) and make the exit code follow the overall verdict. State the compatibility change and the reason. A CI gate must be able to rely on the exit code alone.\n- Fix the walkthrough sentence, so that it says which part the verdict covers.\n\nAcceptance: tests where endpoints are unchanged but the window, a ceiling, or a requirement loosens: each gives a non-contained overall verdict and a nonzero exit. A truly narrowing revision still exits 0.","kind":"bug","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Make diff --containment's verdict cover the whole pass, not only endpoints"}	blake3:qBxTSPFXu3el8ugmGGrm9QejCo9KnDd6c1rKrF8QqWs
1795460008786778	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-da6	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\n`audit-receipts` and `audit-charges` report \"complete\" and \"confirmed\" for debits made under a pass revision that the owner never approved.\n\nEvaluator repro (/home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval4/work/auditdemo):\n1. The owner signs p.toml; the operator runs `verify-approval --archive arch`.\n2. The operator appends an unapproved `[[destination]]` for 203.0.113.99, runs `hallpass archive p.toml arch`, then `spend --n 5 --to 203.0.113.99:443/tcp --record-target`.\n3. The operator restores the signed bytes.\n\n`audit-receipts p.toml arch` then says \"Receipt audit complete ... 203.0.113.99:443/tcp: recorded label extra confirmed\", exit 0, and `audit-charges` says \"complete\".\n\nThe archive does not record HOW bytes arrived: `archive` stores any valid pass, while `verify-approval --archive` stores bytes it verified against an owner signature. No command ties the ledger's digests back to signatures. README says a matching digest identifies bytes, not who approved them, but the audit wording reads as a clean bill of health.\n\nWanted:\n- The archive records provenance per digest: verified by signature (keep the `.sig`, principal and trust-file digest next to the bytes) or archived without one. This must be additive and stay compatible with existing archives, which read as \"without signature\".\n- Both audits report, for each digest the ledger used, whether it was approved by a verified signature. They do not say \"complete\" or \"confirmed\" unless every digest is signature-verified; otherwise they name the unapproved digests and their sequences, as a gap or a separate result. Choose the exit-code behaviour and state it.\n- A simple way to list the digests a ledger used, with first and last sequence and provenance. This could be a field in the audits or `ledger --digests`.\n- Docs: EVIDENCE.md and the auditor's workflow say what \"confirmed\" proves.\n\nAcceptance: the evaluator's scenario, as a test, reports the unapproved digest and does not say complete. A fully signed engagement still audits clean.","kind":"bug","labels":["audit","verification"],"size":"m","title":"Stop audits from confirming debits made under an unapproved pass revision"}	blake3:JXl-Uah4DaPSmMVLAfiTfeQEY3FipVYRX7S1-q5Vvzw
1795460008890549	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-11i	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\nAfter an adoption that may have lost lines (a lost ledger re-created from the witness, or a changed ledger adopted), every `--require-new-ref` spend is refused `reference-inconclusive` for the rest of that ledger (bn-1mk). This is documented and fails closed, but there is no way back:\n- Putting the original backup back afterwards is `ledger-changed`.\n- The reference caller always guards, so it can never spend on that pass again.\n- The only exits are to drop the guard, which loses duplicate protection entirely, or to issue a new pass id.\n\nRepro: spend with references; delete the ledger; `adopt --preview`, then `adopt --reason ... --token ...`; `spend --caller-ref ops-new-1 --require-new-ref` is refused even for a never-seen reference.\n\nWanted: a documented, auditable way for the operator to declare that the lost references were reconciled, after which the guard works again from that point on. For example:\n- `hallpass adopt --references-reconciled MANIFEST` (or a separate command) appends a ledger line recording the manifest's digest: the references from the operator's run record that may be in the lost lines. Guarded spends then refuse those references as duplicates and accept new ones.\n- Or: accept references with a caller-chosen prefix or epoch that is new after the adoption (for example `--ref-epoch`), so that new references cannot collide with lost ones.\n\nChoose the design with a safety argument. A reference that was used in a lost line must never be accepted as new unless the operator explicitly said so in the ledger. Keep the ledger format additive; old binaries must refuse, not misread, the new line, so check how they treat unknown events. Update the reference caller to offer this path, and update the spec and INTEGRATING.md.\n\nAcceptance: a test where, after a lossy adoption and a reconcile step, a new reference is accepted, a reconciled old reference is refused as a duplicate, and a guarded spend before the reconcile step is still inconclusive.","kind":"task","labels":["recovery","accounting"],"size":"m","title":"Let guarded spends resume after the operator reconciles references following a lossy adoption"}	blake3:8mVygcEA9-xTNVWBXC0GdPQCh3mlBvCsWKtIE_JRkJ8
1795460008992548	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-mti	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\nA regression from bn-385. With no `--lock-timeout-ms`, the CLI now always sets the lock-wait callback, and `WriterWait::exclusive` (src/files.rs) then polls `try_lock()` every 20 ms instead of calling the blocking `file.lock()`. Pollers race for the lock rather than being woken by the kernel, so waits get long tails.\n\nEvaluator measurement: 8 writers making 200 spends against a 100k-line ledger (/home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval4/work/conc/race.py) gave 16.5 spends/s in total, p50 65 ms, p99 3.4 s, max 5.5 s; at 10k lines, p99 was 1.1 s. A caller with `--lock-timeout-ms` of about 1–2 s sees spurious `lock-timeout` errors. The bounded-wait path has always polled, with 50 µs to 5 ms sleeps.\n\nWanted:\n- With no limit: take the blocking lock, and fire the notice from a timer, for example a scoped helper thread that waits on a channel with `recv_timeout(LOCK_WAIT_NOTICE_AFTER)` and calls the callback unless the lock was taken first. Look up the holder by path or device and inode from that thread, or pass a duplicated fd. The library still never prints.\n- Consider whether the bounded path can also block in a helper thread up to the limit, rather than poll. If not, document its fairness.\n- Measure p50, p99 and max with 8 writers at 10k and 100k lines, before and after, using the evaluator's race.py or tests/ledger_scale.rs, and put the numbers in DESIGN.md \"Cost per call\", including tail latency and advice on choosing `--lock-timeout-ms`.\n\nAcceptance: the existing lock_and_damage tests pass (notice after about 2 s, holder PID, timeout advice), and the measured p99 at 100k lines drops to the scale of (writers × per-call cost).","kind":"bug","labels":["performance"],"size":"s","title":"Restore fair lock waiting: use a blocking lock with a timer for the wait notice"}	blake3:RxOxkCR87XIL8uLFFrby1RXwWqD3itXip9Bj1NaO_wU
1795460009097606	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ujk	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\nSeveral recovery messages are hard to read or say something false:\n\n1. `ledger-elsewhere` prints one line of about 1,400 characters that repeats the absolute pass and ledger paths five times. Restructure it: a short first sentence naming the situation, then the live ledger and pass file once each, then the steps as a short list, in text mode on several lines. JSON keeps one `error.message`; consider adding structured fields (`live_ledger`, `live_pass`) so the message can refer to them. The read-only variant must still never mention adopt.\n2. The damaged-witness warning and error end with \"Make that directory exist and writable by this user, then run the command again.\" (added in bn-21z). That advice is for a missing or unwritable state directory, not a damaged witness file, whose advice is to move it aside and adopt. Give each cause of `witness-unavailable` only its own next step.\n3. Since bn-20t, the no-witness warning says \"a spend that is allowed records one (a refused or previewed spend does not)\". In fact an ordinary refused spend (for example `ceiling-reached` or `address-not-listed`) does record a witness; only guarded refusals and previews do not. Lead repro: an empty state dir, then `spend ... --n 1000` refused `ceiling-reached`; `st/hallpass/ID.witness` now exists. Make the text true: check exactly which paths record a witness and word it to match.\n4. `adopt --token garbage` and a stale token both say \"stale adoption preview; nothing was recorded\" with code `usage`. Say whether the token is malformed or stale, and give the next step: run `hallpass adopt PASS --preview` again and use its token.\n\nAcceptance: tests for each (message shape and length for ledger-elsewhere, the advice for each witness cause, the witness behaviour of a refused spend against the warning text, both token errors).","kind":"bug","labels":["cli","recovery"],"size":"s","title":"Make recovery messages short and accurate"}	blake3:ydtSsLvI6aeflxlUTXnOG1EGb1Jsm0Y2nu-Ccl5Mfdk
1795460009197938	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-5zp	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\n- A plain `spend PASS COUNTER --to TARGET` (no `--record-target`, no `--caller-ref`) writes a scalar spend line with no target. `audit-receipts` then reports it as a `legacy-spend` gap (\"Gap legacy-spend 1. Sequences [1]\"), even though this version wrote it. The README's basic flow therefore produces no endpoint evidence for an audit.\n- The `archive-missing` gap lists sequence numbers but not the missing digest.\n\nWanted:\n- Decide whether `spend --to` should record its target by default, with an `--no-record-target` opt-out, as the evidence-friendly default. Consider ledger size (see DESIGN.md cost per call), compatibility (old binaries read debit lines already) and privacy. If you change the default, state it as a compatibility change. If not, at least make the README and INTEGRATING.md show `--record-target` where audit evidence is wanted.\n- Rename or explain the `legacy-spend` gap: it means \"a spend line with no recorded target\", not necessarily an old version. Choose a name; if you rename the code, state the compatibility change.\n- `archive-missing` gaps name each missing digest with its sequences, in text and JSON.\n\nAcceptance: tests for the gap wording and the digest listing, and for the new default if you change it.","kind":"task","labels":["audit"],"size":"s","title":"Give audits endpoint evidence by default and make their gaps actionable"}	blake3:QUzbnf8heaaU-cwP7kIJyk_xpLb9EbQEH4H0ViGfh1U
1795460009302142	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1b9	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\nEach verified by the evaluator; the lead re-checked the format-flag one.\n\n1. `--format text --json` silently outputs JSON. A conflicting pair should be a usage error naming both.\n2. For `spend ... --n -5`, clap suggests \"use '-- -5'\", and following the suggestion still fails. Give a clear usage error: N must be a positive integer.\n3. `recording` is `not-recorded` for parser usage errors but `null` for semantic usage errors and for ledger `io` errors. Make it consistent wherever hallpass knows nothing was recorded, and document which errors can have `null`.\n4. Text times: `receipt` and `ledger` print whole seconds, but `audit-receipts` text prints nanoseconds. Pick one rule for text (JSON stays exact) and apply it.\n5. `reconcile` `differences[].expected` and `recorded` are JSON strings (`\"9999\"`) even for numeric fields such as charges, which are numbers elsewhere. Use the field's natural JSON type, document it, and state the compatibility change.\n6. A refused `capacity` says \"Nothing was recorded\". capacity never records, so the phrase is noise there; drop it or say capacity does not record.\n7. In `ledger` text, receipt and note details run together in one unlabelled column. Label them, or give each its own column.\n\nAcceptance: a test per item where behaviour changes.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Tidy small CLI inconsistencies found in the fourth evaluation"}	blake3:bzD1KAIO1vGKItBr8PrnJjhc_pv5BHTgDzt6prJKrWc
1795460009406282	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13t	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\nThree smaller items that need a decision:\n\n1. Pass text fields (`name`, `owner`, `reference`, `operator`, `revoked_reason`) accept U+202E and other bidi controls. They are escaped on display, but `--note` refuses them. Decide whether the pass loader refuses them, as notes do (a compatibility change for existing passes; perhaps `check` refuses them for new passes while old ones keep loading), or whether `lint` warns. Keep display escaping either way.\n2. A name-restriction overlap warning is printed on every `status`, `check` and `diff` call for the whole engagement, with no way to acknowledge it, so operators learn to ignore warnings. Consider an owner-side acknowledgement in the pass (for example a per-destination `overlap_acknowledged = [\"wide\"]`), which turns the warning into a one-line note. It must stay visible in `check` and in `lint`.\n3. Under a default `umask 002`, `hallpass retrieve DIGEST arch > file` gives a 0664 file, which `diff` and `archive` then refuse (with a clear fix-it). Consider `retrieve --output FILE`, which writes 0600 itself, and document it.\n\nAcceptance: tests for whatever behaviour changes, and doc updates (PASS-FORMAT.md, spec.md).","kind":"task","labels":["cli","verification"],"size":"s","title":"Decide on bidi controls in pass text, overlap-warning noise and retrieve file modes"}	blake3:cer8CN4F6EoixxuLu7lJDn18QNjoydzpz8-ErKYVfNQ
1795460009511481	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1y5	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 7/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (c3e3e63) unless noted.\n\n1. docs/WALKTHROUGH.md needs a debug build: it relies on `HALLPASS_UNSAFE_TEST_CLOCK` and `--rehearsal`. A release-binary user must rewrite the window and drop `--rehearsal`. Run that way, it fails closed with \"HALLPASS_UNSAFE_TEST_CLOCK is set but ignored\". Say this at the top and give the exact changes for a live run, or add a short release-binary variant. tests/walkthrough.rs must keep running the main text word for word.\n2. INTEGRATING.md's caller-contract sections (the expected object, the decoder rules) are dense: the evaluator's integrator needed several re-reads. Add a short, complete, runnable minimal integrator, in Python or shell with about 30 lines, that does preflight, spend, the strict reply check and the callback gate. Check it in under docs/, and run it in a test as tests/reference_caller.rs does for the reference caller.\n3. The torn-read and atomic-replace guidance is covered by its own bone; do not duplicate it here.\n\nAcceptance: the new example runs in a test, and the walkthrough test still passes.","kind":"task","labels":["docs"],"size":"s","title":"Make the walkthrough work with a release binary and add a minimal integrator example"}	blake3:nPE8mJi5lwqw6tTxaMIbBwzodkBtP8ymuAxkZhJFxVU
