1795468110706653	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.move	bn-23h	{"state":"done"}	blake3:vljR9ymnARLyiPKLATBCa65tMg2gNz6IVe-NKLdE5Fc
1795470950065959	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ld	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\n**The audit verdict can be forged without a key.** `audit-receipts` and `audit-charges` treat a `DIGEST.approval` record plus `DIGEST.sig` in the archive as \"signature-verified\". Anyone who can write the archive can make an unsigned revision audit `complete`.\n\nLead repro (/home/bob/.cache/hallpass-r5v):\n1. The owner signs eng.pass.toml, then `verify-approval ... --archive arch` (digest D1).\n2. Append `# extra`, run `hallpass archive eng.pass.toml arch` (digest D2, unsigned), and spend under it. `audit-charges` says `incomplete`, as it should.\n3. `cp arch/D1.sig arch/D2.sig`, then write `arch/D2.approval` as a copy of D1's record with `digest` changed to D2 (3 lines of Python).\n4. `audit-charges eng.pass.toml arch` gives \"Charge audit complete ... D2 ... signature-verified for principal owner@example.org\".\n5. `ssh-keygen -Y verify -f signers -I owner@example.org -n hallpass-approval-v1 -s arch/D2.sig < arch/D2` gives \"Could not verify signature.\"\n\nEVIDENCE.md says that audits do not re-run ssh-keygen, but it also recommends a CI gate on `result = \"complete\"`, and no audit option re-verifies.\n\nWanted:\n- `audit-receipts` and `audit-charges` take `--allowed-signers FILE` (the auditor's own trust file). With it, they re-run `ssh-keygen -Y verify` for every recorded signature over the archived bytes, in the `hallpass-approval-v1` namespace, with the recorded principal. Reuse the verify-approval machinery: input_path handling, the bounded stderr and the `Why` reasons.\n  - A failed re-verification is a mismatch, or a gap with its own code, naming the digest. It is never `complete`.\n  - A missing ssh-keygen is an error, not a pass.\n  - Consider `--principal` to require a specific signer.\n- Without `--allowed-signers`, never present a record as verified:\n  - text says \"signature record present (not re-verified)\";\n  - the JSON provenance value distinguishes recorded from re-verified.\n  - The provenance values were added this round and are unreleased, so renaming them is acceptable; state what you chose.\n  - Decide whether `complete` without re-verification stays `complete`; justify it for the CI-gate reader.\n- EVIDENCE.md's CI gate uses `--allowed-signers`, and the auditor's workflow says what each mode proves.\n\nAcceptance:\n- A test of the forgery above: the audit is not complete with `--allowed-signers`, and its text never says \"verified\" without it.\n- A test where a genuinely signed engagement re-verifies clean.\n- Tests skip when ssh-keygen is missing, as the existing verify-approval tests do.","kind":"bug","labels":["audit","verification"],"size":"m","title":"Let audits re-verify archived signatures instead of trusting the signature records"}	blake3:N0hT8DSPSLI_3I5lR64O5sFhWpBL3BgbDEMoVHMAAYg
1795470950292118	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2g2	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\n`spend` and `ledger` text output escape a caller reference twice. A crafted reference then reads like extra receipt fields, for example an out-of-scope target.\n\nLead repro, using the reference `x\", targets \"203.0.113.66:22/tcp\" label \"ssh`:\n- `spend ... --caller-ref \"$R\"` prints `receipt ref \"x\\\\\", targets \\\\\"203.0.113.66:22/tcp\\\\\" label \\\\\"ssh\"`.\n- The `ledger` row prints `receipt: ref \"x\\\\\", targets \\\\\"203.0.113.66:22/tcp\\\\\" label \\\\\"ssh\"`.\n- Read normally, the quoted string ends at `x\\` and a target 203.0.113.66:22/tcp with label ssh seems to follow.\n- `receipt` renders the same reference correctly (`\"x\\\", targets \\\"...\\\" label \\\"ssh\"`), and JSON is correct.\n\nEvaluator script: ref_escaping.py, which also covers `q\"uote` and a backslash.\n\nWanted:\n- One escaping helper for quoted values in text output, used by spend, ledger, receipt, audit-receipts and reconcile. A value is escaped exactly once.\n- Check every text renderer that quotes caller-supplied text: references, notes, labels and names.\n\nAcceptance: tests for the three references above, checking the spend text, the ledger text and the receipt text against one expected rendering.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Escape caller references once in spend and ledger text"}	blake3:Ef8AeNY3xbKWB-vAIIkwnp5w9RlqPQrelXxp8k0UO3I
1795470950500594	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1tt	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\nPASS-FORMAT.md says a caller reference \"excludes control characters and Unicode formatting and line controls that disguise text\". Yet Unicode format characters (general category Cf) are accepted and printed raw:\n- U+200B, U+200D, U+2060, U+FEFF, U+00AD, U+FFF9–FFFB, and tag characters U+E0000–E007F.\n- They are accepted in caller references, notes and pass text fields (`owner`, `reference` and the others).\n- Leading and trailing spaces are accepted in caller references.\n\nLead repro:\n- `spend ... --caller-ref $'ref\\u200b1'` is RECORDED, and so is `--caller-ref $'tag\\U000E0065'`.\n- A pass with `owner = \"Pat\\u200b Owner\"` passes `check`.\n- `diff` prints `approval.owner: Pat Owner -> Pat Owner [review required]`: the two values look identical.\n\nEvaluator scripts: hostile_text.py, invisible_diff.py and fmt_probe.py.\n- `approval-id` differs for passes that look identical.\n- `status` shows `reference: email` while the bytes carry a hidden word.\n- `ref1` and `ref<U+200B>1` are different references for `--require-new-ref`.\n\nWanted:\n- Extend the \"disguising\" set (`is_disguising` in src/ledger.rs, used by notes and, since bn-13t, by pass text) to every Cf character. That covers the zero-width characters, soft hyphen, BOM, interlinear annotation and tag characters, along with the current bidi and line separators. Use a small hand-written range table, no new dependency; add a test that pins the table.\n- Caller references: refuse Cf characters and leading or trailing whitespace.\n- Compatibility: references and notes are refused only as new input. A ledger written earlier that holds such a reference must still read, not become `ledger-corrupt`, so check where `valid_text` is used when reading lines, and escape such characters on display (as `\\u{200b}`). Passes are refused at load, as bidi controls are, and the change is stated in your report.\n- Escape Cf characters in all text output that shows caller- or pass-supplied text, so an existing value can never display as something else.\n\nAcceptance: tests for each class (zero-width, BOM, soft hyphen, tag characters, leading or trailing space in a reference) on new input, a test that an old ledger line holding such a reference still reads and displays escaped, and a test that a pass holding one is refused.","kind":"bug","labels":["cli","verification"],"size":"s","title":"Refuse invisible Unicode format characters in references, notes and pass text"}	blake3:SGwYJJrtHMpGxrcMvML7vb5pdr1-1TzYW2yAHibiJy0
1795470950691603	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6d1	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\nINTEGRATING.md (around line 207) says throughput \"fell from about 2,400 spends a second on a small ledger\", and around line 212 that \"each call takes a few milliseconds\". Those are tmpfs figures; DESIGN.md says so (about line 508, \"the sync after each append costs nothing here\") and adds \"+7 ms on ext4 NVMe\", but INTEGRATING.md does not.\n\nThe evaluator measured on this machine's ext4 disk (perf_small.py and `perf.py real 3000`):\n- a read-only call: about 0.7 ms (matches);\n- a spend: 13.5–18 ms (bound by fsync);\n- 3,000 sequential guarded spends: 74.9 s, about 40 a second;\n- 8 writers on a one-line ledger: about 40 spends a second.\n\nWanted:\n- Re-measure with a release build on the home disk (ext4), on a small ledger and at 10k and 100k lines, single writer and 8 writers. Use the evaluator's scripts or tests/ledger_scale.rs, and a separate XDG_STATE_HOME on the same disk.\n- Give INTEGRATING.md and DESIGN.md \"Cost per call\" disk-backed numbers, keeping tmpfs numbers only where they are labelled as such. Say plainly that a spend is dominated by fsync, about 15 ms on this disk, so one process does about 60 spends a second.\n- Check the batching advice still holds with those numbers.\n\nAcceptance: every throughput figure in README, INTEGRATING.md and DESIGN.md names the storage it was measured on, and the numbers match a re-run.","kind":"task","labels":["docs","performance"],"size":"s","title":"Make INTEGRATING.md's cost figures match a real disk"}	blake3:KGRjS_olvfV-e-9AESPE4joyylRCQKQZR5ASB_IRP58
1795470950904394	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1cz	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\nA typo in the window is accepted silently. `valid_until = 2062-11-30T17:00:00Z` (meant to be 2026) passes `check` with exit 0 and no warning, and `status` shows \"13155 d 19 h left\". An already-ended window also passes `check` with no warning. DESIGN.md lists \"mistakes in the approval record\" as the first threat, and network breadth is capped, yet the window gets no warning at all.\n\nLead repro: copy a pass, change only `valid_until` to 2062, and run `check`: OK, exit 0, no warning.\n\nWanted:\n- Warnings (not refusals: the window is the owner's decision) in `check`, `status` and `lint`, each with a code and documented in spec.md:\n  - a window longer than a threshold; choose it and justify it, for example 90 days;\n  - a window that has already ended, or starts far in the future (check knows the current time; say which clock it uses);\n  - optionally, `valid_from` far before the approval reference date, if that can be done reliably.\n- Keep `check`'s exit code; a warning only.\n- `diff` already flags window changes for review; make sure the warning also shows for NEW there.\n\nAcceptance: tests for a long window, an ended window and a normal window (no warning), in text and JSON.","kind":"task","labels":["cli","approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Warn about an implausible approval window in check, status and lint"}	blake3:UCUy6_hCIo4WmpwjIQfOBTU_VBUtK0RBjgCaedCVRWE
1795470951102976	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1ab	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\n`diff --containment` cannot approve a revocation. `revoked: false -> true` gives \"Endpoint scope ...: inconclusive (both-windows-must-be-open)\", \"Overall verdict: inconclusive\" and exit 2. A CI gate that requires `contained` therefore blocks the most restrictive change possible.\n\nLead repro: two copies of a pass differing only in `revoked = true`; `diff r0.toml r1.toml --containment --at 2026-11-20T09:00:00Z` exits 2, inconclusive.\n\nWanted:\n- When NEW admits nothing at the instant (revoked, or its window does not cover it), the endpoint result is `contained`, with a reason such as `new-admits-nothing`: every endpoint NEW admits is, vacuously, admitted by OLD.\n- Non-endpoint loosenings are still judged as today, so the overall verdict is `widened` if, say, a ceiling was also raised.\n- Keep `inconclusive` for OLD closed and NEW open: OLD admits nothing, so containment can't be shown.\n- Update spec.md and the help text.\n\nAcceptance: tests that a pure revocation, and a window moved so that NEW is closed at T, give `contained` and exit 0; that a revocation plus a raised ceiling gives `widened`; and that OLD closed with NEW open stays `inconclusive`.","kind":"bug","labels":["approval-review"],"size":"s","title":"Treat a revoked or closed NEW revision as contained in diff --containment"}	blake3:MHq4Hy_V1suvNrCPuiRKsEeqc7-xyCpzkIJyCgogjTM
1795470951277993	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-26g	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\nhallpass refuses a pass or ledger file that others can write. It says nothing about the directory that holds them. In a directory writable by others without the sticky bit, another user can replace the pass, the ledger or the lock file by `rename`, which bypasses the file-mode checks.\n\nLead repro: `chmod 777` a directory, put a 0600 pass in it, and `spend` is RECORDED with no warning.\n\nWanted:\n- Check the pass's directory, which is also the ledger's. If another user can write it and it lacks the sticky bit, warn in `check`, `status` and `spend`, or refuse writes. Decide, and justify it against the existing file-mode policy in src/files.rs and the threat model in DESIGN.md.\n  - If you refuse: give a clear fix-it (`chmod o-w,g-w DIR`), and consider whether group-writable counts, as it does for the pass file.\n  - If you warn: give it a code, document it, and test it.\n- Consider the state directory: XDG_STATE_HOME/hallpass is already checked; confirm this.\n\nAcceptance: tests for a world-writable directory, a sticky world-writable directory, and a normal directory.","kind":"bug","labels":["verification"],"size":"s","title":"Warn when the pass or ledger directory is writable by other users"}	blake3:CyTURR7y2Z2SrnaraLMAHbCPTZjhzQ8Xn-_pk5lX1Sc
1795470951456636	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2l8	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\nWALKTHROUGH.md step 8 says \"Hand over the journal, the outbox, the pass with its ledger, the archive and the handoff output\". It leaves out two consequences that the evaluator hit (see the threat model):\n1. A colleague who copies the directory and uses their own state directory has no witness. Every guarded unit is then `reference-inconclusive` until they `adopt`. The evaluator's `blair-s2/u1` was refused.\n2. The original copy stays spendable. The evaluator's first operator recorded ledger entry 7 in his copy after the colleague recorded entry 8 in hers, giving two independent budgets for one approval.\n\nWanted:\n- Step 8 says how to hand over safely:\n  - the same account and state directory, or\n  - a move (not a copy) followed by `adopt` on the new account, with the exact commands and what they print.\n- It says that the old copy must not be used after the move. Check what hallpass does on the old account after a move: the ledger is gone but the witness remains, so it should be refused; check that and quote it.\n- If a step is added to the walkthrough, tests/walkthrough.rs must still run it word for word. The second account can be simulated with a second XDG_STATE_HOME.\n- Also mention it in INTEGRATING.md or the reference caller README where handoff is described.\n\nAcceptance: the walkthrough test passes with the new text, and every claim in it is shown by a command on the page.","kind":"task","labels":["docs","recovery"],"size":"s","title":"Say in the walkthrough what handing a pass to another account involves"}	blake3:6t2hEEHVST5ES4V6UAv4lyLZrhCcx1-R3Y_ku7be8PY
1795470951640906	hallpass-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-rzo	{"description":"Found in an independent evaluation on 2026-11-23 (score 8/10) and reproduced by the lead on main (35abc54) unless noted. Evaluator scripts: /home/bob/.cache/hallpass-eval5/work/scripts/.\n\nSmall items, each reproduced by the evaluator; the lead re-checked those marked (L).\n\n1. (L) `status` on an over-ceiling ledger always says \"the ceiling was lowered below what is spent\" (src/main.rs around line 944), even when that is not why. Evaluator script: audit_mismatch.py, a ledger forged over the ceiling. Say what is known: spent exceeds the ceiling. Mention the lowered-ceiling case only as one possibility, or tell the cases apart if the ledger shows it.\n2. A ledger path that is a directory or FIFO gets the advice \"give the path of the pass file\", which is wrong advice there. Give advice for a ledger that is not a regular file.\n3. (L) `audit-charges` prints the no-witness warning twice. Print each warning once; check `audit-receipts` and the other evidence commands too.\n4. `docs/conformance/check.py` prints nothing on success. Print one summary line, for example \"N cases passed\", and keep the exit code.\n5. (L) Running `minimal_caller.py` (and the test that runs it) leaves `docs/conformance/__pycache__` in the checkout. It is ignored by git, but writing into the docs tree is untidy. Set `sys.dont_write_bytecode = True` before the import in minimal_caller.py, and `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` in the test harnesses that run Python (tests/minimal_integrator.rs, tests/reference_caller*.rs).\n6. (L) `--to 192.0.2.10:443/tcp@` says only \"--to: the name is empty\". Add the fix: drop the `@` or write the name after it, for example `192.0.2.10:443/tcp@app.example.com`.\n\nAcceptance: a test per behaviour change.","kind":"task","labels":["cli"],"size":"s","title":"Polish messages and tooling found in the fifth evaluation"}	blake3:4aFTFfAEt0YTVVn984qbbPbFtM2jyY38Qv1mAbM1elo
